window.PREP_DATA = window.PREP_DATA || {};
window.PREP_DATA["workforce"] = [{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Fish stocks in the North Sea are nearly exhausted.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — the passage only says the EU wants to preserve stocks and environmentalists want to avoid depletion. It never says how large or small the stocks currently are.","passage":"In an effort to preserve fishing stocks, the European Union uses a quota system to regulate the amount of fish that can be taken out of the North Sea. This involves defining the tonnage of the various types of North Sea fish that each EU member country can take. As soon as the catch is landed — but not before — fishermen can be fined for landing more fish at their port than their quota permits. Each year fishermen dump thousands of tonnes of fish back into the sea either because they have reached their quota and don’t want to be fined or because the fish are too small to be caught legally. Some environmental groups feel that existing approaches are not working since these fish are already dead and throwing them back serves no real purpose. They believe that other steps need to be taken to conserve North Sea fishing stocks and avoid depletion.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-0"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Some environmental groups think new fish conservation methods are required.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — they believe “existing approaches are not working” and that “other steps need to be taken”, i.e. new methods.","passage":"In an effort to preserve fishing stocks, the European Union uses a quota system to regulate the amount of fish that can be taken out of the North Sea. This involves defining the tonnage of the various types of North Sea fish that each EU member country can take. As soon as the catch is landed — but not before — fishermen can be fined for landing more fish at their port than their quota permits. Each year fishermen dump thousands of tonnes of fish back into the sea either because they have reached their quota and don’t want to be fined or because the fish are too small to be caught legally. Some environmental groups feel that existing approaches are not working since these fish are already dead and throwing them back serves no real purpose. They believe that other steps need to be taken to conserve North Sea fishing stocks and avoid depletion.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-1"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Fishermen can be fined for catching too many fish.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"False — fishermen can be fined only “as soon as the catch is landed — but not before”, so they are fined for landing too many fish, not for catching them.","passage":"In an effort to preserve fishing stocks, the European Union uses a quota system to regulate the amount of fish that can be taken out of the North Sea. This involves defining the tonnage of the various types of North Sea fish that each EU member country can take. As soon as the catch is landed — but not before — fishermen can be fined for landing more fish at their port than their quota permits. Each year fishermen dump thousands of tonnes of fish back into the sea either because they have reached their quota and don’t want to be fined or because the fish are too small to be caught legally. Some environmental groups feel that existing approaches are not working since these fish are already dead and throwing them back serves no real purpose. They believe that other steps need to be taken to conserve North Sea fishing stocks and avoid depletion.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-2"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"The Phoenix Mars Lander has provided proof that life once existed on Mars.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — scientists only speculate that the patches were water ice. The passage does not say what Phoenix has proved or failed to prove overall.","passage":"Nobody knows what life forms may exist outside our own planet. The search for extra-terrestrial life in the universe took a step nearer to fruition with the discovery in June of what are believed to be traces of water on the surface of Mars. Life on our planet requires water and its presence on Mars may point towards the existence of past life on the planet. The Phoenix Mars Lander robot landed on the plains of Mars on May 25 2008, searching for signs that the Martian environment might once have been habitable to life. When it dug a ditch in the planet’s surface, photos revealed small patches of bright material. Four days later those patches had disappeared, causing scientists to speculate that they were water ice that had previously been buried and which vaporised when exposed to the air. Scientists insisted that if the patches had been salt, they wouldn’t have disappeared and if they had been solid carbon dioxide, then they wouldn’t have vaporised.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-3"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Life forms on Mars require water in order to survive.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — the passage says life on our planet requires water and that nobody knows what life forms exist elsewhere.","passage":"Nobody knows what life forms may exist outside our own planet. The search for extra-terrestrial life in the universe took a step nearer to fruition with the discovery in June of what are believed to be traces of water on the surface of Mars. Life on our planet requires water and its presence on Mars may point towards the existence of past life on the planet. The Phoenix Mars Lander robot landed on the plains of Mars on May 25 2008, searching for signs that the Martian environment might once have been habitable to life. When it dug a ditch in the planet’s surface, photos revealed small patches of bright material. Four days later those patches had disappeared, causing scientists to speculate that they were water ice that had previously been buried and which vaporised when exposed to the air. Scientists insisted that if the patches had been salt, they wouldn’t have disappeared and if they had been solid carbon dioxide, then they wouldn’t have vaporised.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-4"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Since the Phoenix Mars Lander cannot excavate, it is limited to surface photography.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"False — Phoenix “dug a ditch in the planet’s surface”, so it can excavate.","passage":"Nobody knows what life forms may exist outside our own planet. The search for extra-terrestrial life in the universe took a step nearer to fruition with the discovery in June of what are believed to be traces of water on the surface of Mars. Life on our planet requires water and its presence on Mars may point towards the existence of past life on the planet. The Phoenix Mars Lander robot landed on the plains of Mars on May 25 2008, searching for signs that the Martian environment might once have been habitable to life. When it dug a ditch in the planet’s surface, photos revealed small patches of bright material. Four days later those patches had disappeared, causing scientists to speculate that they were water ice that had previously been buried and which vaporised when exposed to the air. Scientists insisted that if the patches had been salt, they wouldn’t have disappeared and if they had been solid carbon dioxide, then they wouldn’t have vaporised.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-5"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"A level results are improving year-on-year.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — only 2007 and 2008 are compared; nothing is said about earlier years.","passage":"The 2008 A level results show that 97.2% of students passed compared with 96.6% in 2007. And 25.9% gained A grades, a rise of 0.6% from the 2007 results. The number of students sitting A levels in 2008 was also up, at a record high of 827,737. This high success-rate is causing concerns in some quarters that the exams are getting easier. However the government attributes the annual change to the increased spending on schools over the period. Meanwhile the general secretary of the Association of Teachers and Lecturers, Dr Mary Bousted, has berated teaching methods in schools as spoon-feeding students to pass exams without developing the desire to continue learning or gaining the skills necessary to learn independently. Adding to the debate, the general secretary of the University and College Union, Sally Hunt, suggested it is unfair to downplay the students’ results and the hard work of their teachers.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-6"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"A level exams are getting easier.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — some people are concerned the exams are getting easier, but no evidence either way is given.","passage":"The 2008 A level results show that 97.2% of students passed compared with 96.6% in 2007. And 25.9% gained A grades, a rise of 0.6% from the 2007 results. The number of students sitting A levels in 2008 was also up, at a record high of 827,737. This high success-rate is causing concerns in some quarters that the exams are getting easier. However the government attributes the annual change to the increased spending on schools over the period. Meanwhile the general secretary of the Association of Teachers and Lecturers, Dr Mary Bousted, has berated teaching methods in schools as spoon-feeding students to pass exams without developing the desire to continue learning or gaining the skills necessary to learn independently. Adding to the debate, the general secretary of the University and College Union, Sally Hunt, suggested it is unfair to downplay the students’ results and the hard work of their teachers.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-7"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"More money was spent on schools in 2008 than in 2007.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — the government attributes the change to “the increased spending on schools over the period”, i.e. 2007 to 2008.","passage":"The 2008 A level results show that 97.2% of students passed compared with 96.6% in 2007. And 25.9% gained A grades, a rise of 0.6% from the 2007 results. The number of students sitting A levels in 2008 was also up, at a record high of 827,737. This high success-rate is causing concerns in some quarters that the exams are getting easier. However the government attributes the annual change to the increased spending on schools over the period. Meanwhile the general secretary of the Association of Teachers and Lecturers, Dr Mary Bousted, has berated teaching methods in schools as spoon-feeding students to pass exams without developing the desire to continue learning or gaining the skills necessary to learn independently. Adding to the debate, the general secretary of the University and College Union, Sally Hunt, suggested it is unfair to downplay the students’ results and the hard work of their teachers.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-8"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Second-hand smoking can cause lung cancer and other respiratory diseases.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — the passage mentions the “effects of secondhand smoking” but never says what they are. Do not use outside knowledge.","passage":"On 1st July 2007 the smoke-free law was introduced in England with the aim of protecting employees in their places of work, and the general public in enclosed public places, from the effects of secondhand smoking. These law changes mean that all public transport and work vehicles used by more than one person must be smoke-free, no-smoking signs must be displayed in all smoke-free premises and vehicles, and staff smoking rooms are no longer allowed; staff who want to smoke must go outside. Local councils in England have the responsibility of enforcing the law but it is the legal responsibility of the managers of smoke-free premises and vehicles to make sure people don’t smoke. Individuals can be fined or prosecuted for breaking the law and employers can be fined if they do not display the no-smoking signs or if they allow smoking to continue on their premises.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-9"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"The smoke-free law had the public’s welfare in mind.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — the law aimed to protect employees “and the general public in enclosed public places”.","passage":"On 1st July 2007 the smoke-free law was introduced in England with the aim of protecting employees in their places of work, and the general public in enclosed public places, from the effects of secondhand smoking. These law changes mean that all public transport and work vehicles used by more than one person must be smoke-free, no-smoking signs must be displayed in all smoke-free premises and vehicles, and staff smoking rooms are no longer allowed; staff who want to smoke must go outside. Local councils in England have the responsibility of enforcing the law but it is the legal responsibility of the managers of smoke-free premises and vehicles to make sure people don’t smoke. Individuals can be fined or prosecuted for breaking the law and employers can be fined if they do not display the no-smoking signs or if they allow smoking to continue on their premises.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-10"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"It is no longer legal for smoking rooms to be provided for employees inside buildings.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — “staff smoking rooms are no longer allowed; staff who want to smoke must go outside.”","passage":"On 1st July 2007 the smoke-free law was introduced in England with the aim of protecting employees in their places of work, and the general public in enclosed public places, from the effects of secondhand smoking. These law changes mean that all public transport and work vehicles used by more than one person must be smoke-free, no-smoking signs must be displayed in all smoke-free premises and vehicles, and staff smoking rooms are no longer allowed; staff who want to smoke must go outside. Local councils in England have the responsibility of enforcing the law but it is the legal responsibility of the managers of smoke-free premises and vehicles to make sure people don’t smoke. Individuals can be fined or prosecuted for breaking the law and employers can be fined if they do not display the no-smoking signs or if they allow smoking to continue on their premises.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-11"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Girls who attend single-sex schools perform better in maths and sciences than their counterparts in co-educational schools.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — single-sex schools “subvert stereotypical course-taking patterns and results”, but no subject-specific results are given, and experts call the research inconclusive.","passage":"The merits of single-sex education have long been debated in the United States, where demand for single-sex schools is on the rise. Title IV, a 1972 law prohibiting sex discrimination in education, was amended in 2006, allowing for the establishment of single-sex state schools so long as a co-educational alternative is available. While critics view single-sex schools as discriminatory and inadequate preparation for adult life, advocates claim that children, and particularly girls, benefit from a single-sex education. Some American research shows that girls attending single-sex schools have higher self-esteem, participate more in class, and score higher on aptitude tests than their counterparts in co-educational schools. A 2005 study claimed that both girls and boys attending single-sex schools spent more time on homework and had fewer disciplinary problems. Single-sex schools subvert stereotypical course-taking patterns and results. Advocates of single-sex schooling argue that educators can teach more effectively by tailoring their tuition to reflect current research about gender-based brain development. Many experts, however, believe that research into single-sex education is inconclusive, and that so long as the education provided is gender-fair, both girls and boys can thrive in a co-educational environment.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-12"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"The trend towards American single-sex state education is a relatively recent phenomenon.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — single-sex state schools only became permissible after Title IV was amended in 2006.","passage":"The merits of single-sex education have long been debated in the United States, where demand for single-sex schools is on the rise. Title IV, a 1972 law prohibiting sex discrimination in education, was amended in 2006, allowing for the establishment of single-sex state schools so long as a co-educational alternative is available. While critics view single-sex schools as discriminatory and inadequate preparation for adult life, advocates claim that children, and particularly girls, benefit from a single-sex education. Some American research shows that girls attending single-sex schools have higher self-esteem, participate more in class, and score higher on aptitude tests than their counterparts in co-educational schools. A 2005 study claimed that both girls and boys attending single-sex schools spent more time on homework and had fewer disciplinary problems. Single-sex schools subvert stereotypical course-taking patterns and results. Advocates of single-sex schooling argue that educators can teach more effectively by tailoring their tuition to reflect current research about gender-based brain development. Many experts, however, believe that research into single-sex education is inconclusive, and that so long as the education provided is gender-fair, both girls and boys can thrive in a co-educational environment.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-13"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Proponents of single-sex education believe there are neurological differences between the two genders.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — advocates want teaching tailored to “current research about gender-based brain development”.","passage":"The merits of single-sex education have long been debated in the United States, where demand for single-sex schools is on the rise. Title IV, a 1972 law prohibiting sex discrimination in education, was amended in 2006, allowing for the establishment of single-sex state schools so long as a co-educational alternative is available. While critics view single-sex schools as discriminatory and inadequate preparation for adult life, advocates claim that children, and particularly girls, benefit from a single-sex education. Some American research shows that girls attending single-sex schools have higher self-esteem, participate more in class, and score higher on aptitude tests than their counterparts in co-educational schools. A 2005 study claimed that both girls and boys attending single-sex schools spent more time on homework and had fewer disciplinary problems. Single-sex schools subvert stereotypical course-taking patterns and results. Advocates of single-sex schooling argue that educators can teach more effectively by tailoring their tuition to reflect current research about gender-based brain development. Many experts, however, believe that research into single-sex education is inconclusive, and that so long as the education provided is gender-fair, both girls and boys can thrive in a co-educational environment.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-14"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"In Los Angeles, a political candidate who buys saturation radio advertising will get maximum name recognition.\n\nThe statement above logically conveys which of the following?","opts":["Radio advertising is the most important factor in political campaigns in Los Angeles.","Maximum name recognition in Los Angeles will help a candidate to win a higher percentage of votes cast in the city.","Saturation radio advertising reaches every demographically distinct sector of the voting population of Los Angeles.","For maximum name recognition a candidate need not spend on media channels other than radio advertising.","A candidate’s record of achievement in the Los Angeles area will do little to affect his or her name recognition there."],"ans":3,"exp":"The statement says saturation radio advertising is sufficient for maximum name recognition. So a candidate need not spend on any other media to achieve it (D). The other options claim things about importance, votes, demographics or track record that the statement never addresses.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-15"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"The rate of violent crime in this state is up 30 percent from last year. The fault lies entirely in our court system: recently our judges’ sentences have been so lenient that criminals can now do almost anything without fear of a long prison term.\n\nThe argument above would be weakened if it were true that","opts":["85 percent of the other states in the nation have lower crime rates than does this state.","White-collar crime in this state has also increased by over 25 percent in the last year.","35 percent of the police in this state have been laid off in the last year due to budget cuts.","Polls show that 65 percent of the population in this state opposes capital punishment.","The state has hired 25 new judges in the last year to compensate for deaths and retirements."],"ans":2,"exp":"The argument blames the rise “entirely” on lenient courts. A large police lay-off (C) is an alternative cause of rising crime, which weakens that claim. A compares states (out of scope), B is consistent with the argument, D is irrelevant, and E could even strengthen it if the new judges are lenient.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-16"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"The increase in the number of newspaper articles exposed as fabrications serves to bolster the contention that publishers are more interested in boosting circulation than in printing the truth. Even minor publications have staffs to check such obvious fraud.\n\nThe argument above assumes that","opts":["Newspaper stories exposed as fabrications are a recent phenomenon.","Everything a newspaper prints must be factually verifiable.","Fact checking is more comprehensive for minor publications than for major ones.","Only recently have newspapers admitted to publishing intentionally fraudulent stories.","The publishers of newspapers are the people who decide what to print in their newspapers."],"ans":4,"exp":"The argument blames publishers for printed fabrications. That only works if publishers are the ones deciding what gets printed (E); if editors or others decided, the conclusion about publishers’ motives would collapse.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-17"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Time and again it has been shown that students who attend colleges with low faculty/student ratios get the most well-rounded education. As a result, when my children are ready to attend college, I’ll be sure they attend a school with a very small student population.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, identifies the greatest flaw in the reasoning above?","opts":["A low faculty/student ratio is the effect of a well-rounded education, not its source.","Intelligence should be considered the result of childhood environment, not advanced education.","A very small student population does not, by itself, ensure a low faculty/student ratio.","Parental desires and preferences rarely determine a child’s choice of a college or university.","Students must take advantage of the low faculty/student ratio by intentionally choosing small classes."],"ans":2,"exp":"The evidence is about a low faculty/student ratio, but the conclusion switches to a small student population. A small college can still have few staff per student, so C exposes the gap.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-18"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"All German philosophers, except for Marx, are idealists.\n\nFrom which of the following can the statement above be most properly inferred?","opts":["Except for Marx, if someone is an idealist philosopher, then he or she is German.","Marx is the only non-German philosopher who is an idealist.","If a German is an idealist, then he or she is a philosopher, as long as he or she is not Marx.","Marx is not an idealist German philosopher.","Aside from the philosopher Marx, if someone is a German philosopher, then he or she is an idealist."],"ans":4,"exp":"E is a restatement of the claim in if–then form: any German philosopher other than Marx is an idealist. A and C reverse the conditional, B is about non-German philosophers, and D only says Marx is the exception without establishing the rule for everyone else.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-19"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":4,"exp":"Rule 1: cross-hatching is added to the circle below the shaded one (wrapping to the top) and removed from a circle that has been hatched for two consecutive figures. Rule 2: the middle circle alternates between medium size and largest. Rule 3: the hatching slopes upwards for two figures, then downwards for two. Applying all three gives figure E.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_q21.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-20"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":0,"exp":"Rule 1: the triangle with a dot alternates between being mirrored in the top-left→bottom-right diagonal and the top-right→bottom-left diagonal. Rule 2: the rectangle alternates between vertical and horizontal mirroring. Rule 3: the rectangle grows wider by the same amount each time, away from the nearest edge. Only A satisfies all three.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_q22.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-21"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":3,"exp":"Rule 1: the small triangle nearest the bottom-right corner is split into two equal triangles each time. Rule 2: the two newest triangles are unshaded; the rest alternate shaded/unshaded each image. Rule 3: the stick figure climbs the hypotenuse of the largest triangle by an equal step. That gives D.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_q23.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-22"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":4,"exp":"Rule 1: the shading of the small triangles moves three triangles anticlockwise each time. Rule 2: the cross-hatched kite shapes (hatched towards the nearest corner) move clockwise, alternately by one and two positions. Rule 3: the centre shape alternates between downward- and upward-sloping hatching. That gives E.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_q24.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-23"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":3,"exp":"Rule 1: two bricks are added each time from the centre outwards, completing each row before starting the next. Rule 2: bricks on the bottom-left→top-right diagonal have upward hatching. Rule 3: the circle alternates between central whole bricks and far-right whole bricks. Rule 4: downward hatching moves to the 1st, 2nd, then 3rd full brick of each row, repeating. That gives D.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_q25.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-24"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":3,"exp":"Rule 1: the shapes move one corner anticlockwise each time. Rule 2: the shapes alternate between circle and square. Rule 3: hatching follows off, off, on, on… Rule 4: sizes cycle small, medium, large. That gives D.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_q26.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-25"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":0,"exp":"Rule 1: the shapes are positioned where the triangle in the previous item was pointing. Rule 2: the shape inside the triangle cycles circle, square, star. Rule 3: the shading alternates between the middle shape and the triangle. Rule 4: sizes cycle small, medium, large. That gives A.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_q27.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-26"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":2,"exp":"Rule 1: the number of stars at top right predicts the number of horizontal lines in the next box. Rule 2: circles alternate with triangles. Rule 3: the number of circles or triangles is the sum of the two previous boxes (some are hidden by the black shading). Only C fits; the other options break at least one rule.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_q28.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-27"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":0,"exp":"Rule 1: the scales tip left, straight, right, straight, repeating. Rule 2: squares weigh more than triangles and circles, which weigh the same. Rule 3: the support alternates between downward and upward cross-hatching. Rule 4: any triangles being weighed have the opposite hatching to the support. That gives A.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_q29.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-28"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":2,"exp":"Rule 1: downward hatching moves top-left, bottom-right, bottom-left, top-right, repeating. Rule 2: upward hatching moves top-right, bottom-left, bottom-right, repeating. Rule 3: the arrow points to where the small circle will be next. Rule 4: the square takes the small circle’s previous position. That gives C.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_q30.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-29"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"How much V.A.T will Annex & Co pay on its services if it is registered with VISCO Tax Company and made a profit of 10% on services in June?","opts":["₦1,050","₦950","₦850","₦750","₦650"],"ans":3,"exp":"Profit on services = 10% × ₦50,000 = ₦5,000. Registered with VISCO, so the rate is 15%: 15% × ₦5,000 = ₦750.","passage":"Every business organisation operating in Nigeria must pay V.A.T (Value Added Tax) of 17.5% on the profit it makes on the products it sells or services it renders. However, an organisation registered with VISCO Tax Company is partly exempted and pays a tax of 15% instead.\n\nIncome for June 2013:\n| Company | Durables (₦) | Perishables (₦) | Services (₦) |\n| Annex & Co | 150,000 | 175,000 | 50,000 |\n| Bisco Solutions | 125,000 | 180,000 | 25,000 |\n| Casper Nig. Ltd | 85,000 | 200,000 | 40,000 |","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-30"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Bisco Solutions is not registered with VISCO Tax Company and made a profit equal to half its perishables income. How much V.A.T would Bisco pay on its perishable goods?","opts":["₦14,000","₦15,000","₦15,300","₦15,500","₦16,000"],"ans":2,"exp":"Source working: profit = ½ × ₦180,000 = ₦90,000, and 17% × ₦90,000 = ₦15,300.","passage":"Every business organisation operating in Nigeria must pay V.A.T (Value Added Tax) of 17.5% on the profit it makes on the products it sells or services it renders. However, an organisation registered with VISCO Tax Company is partly exempted and pays a tax of 15% instead.\n\nIncome for June 2013:\n| Company | Durables (₦) | Perishables (₦) | Services (₦) |\n| Annex & Co | 150,000 | 175,000 | 50,000 |\n| Bisco Solutions | 125,000 | 180,000 | 25,000 |\n| Casper Nig. Ltd | 85,000 | 200,000 | 40,000 |","note":"The source answer key is used here. It applies 17%, although the passage states 17.5% for unregistered firms; at 17.5% the answer would be ₦15,750, which is not an option.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-31"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"All three companies are owned by ABC & Co Nig Ltd, which is registered with VISCO Tax Company. How much V.A.T would ABC & Co pay on its combined services income if it made a profit of 40%?","opts":["₦17,250","₦17,000","₦16,500","₦16,250","₦16,000"],"ans":0,"exp":"Source working: combined services income = 50,000 + 25,000 + 40,000 = ₦115,000, and 15% × ₦115,000 = ₦17,250.","passage":"Every business organisation operating in Nigeria must pay V.A.T (Value Added Tax) of 17.5% on the profit it makes on the products it sells or services it renders. However, an organisation registered with VISCO Tax Company is partly exempted and pays a tax of 15% instead.\n\nIncome for June 2013:\n| Company | Durables (₦) | Perishables (₦) | Services (₦) |\n| Annex & Co | 150,000 | 175,000 | 50,000 |\n| Bisco Solutions | 125,000 | 180,000 | 25,000 |\n| Casper Nig. Ltd | 85,000 | 200,000 | 40,000 |","note":"The source answer key is used here. Worked independently, V.A.T in this passage is charged on profit: 40% × ₦115,000 = ₦46,000, and 15% × ₦46,000 = ₦6,900, which is not an option.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-32"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Casper earned its services income without incurring any cost. If Casper is registered with VISCO Tax Company, how much V.A.T would it pay on services?","opts":["₦3,000","₦4,000","₦5,000","₦5,500","₦6,000"],"ans":4,"exp":"With no costs, the whole ₦40,000 services income is profit. Registered rate 15%: 15% × 40,000 = ₦6,000.","passage":"Every business organisation operating in Nigeria must pay V.A.T (Value Added Tax) of 17.5% on the profit it makes on the products it sells or services it renders. However, an organisation registered with VISCO Tax Company is partly exempted and pays a tax of 15% instead.\n\nIncome for June 2013:\n| Company | Durables (₦) | Perishables (₦) | Services (₦) |\n| Annex & Co | 150,000 | 175,000 | 50,000 |\n| Bisco Solutions | 125,000 | 180,000 | 25,000 |\n| Casper Nig. Ltd | 85,000 | 200,000 | 40,000 |","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-33"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"How much V.A.T would Annex & Co pay on its durable goods if it is not registered with VISCO Tax Company and its June profit on durables was ₦100,000?","opts":["₦12,800","₦14,000","₦17,000","₦18,280","₦19,000"],"ans":2,"exp":"Source working: 17% × ₦100,000 = ₦17,000.","passage":"Every business organisation operating in Nigeria must pay V.A.T (Value Added Tax) of 17.5% on the profit it makes on the products it sells or services it renders. However, an organisation registered with VISCO Tax Company is partly exempted and pays a tax of 15% instead.\n\nIncome for June 2013:\n| Company | Durables (₦) | Perishables (₦) | Services (₦) |\n| Annex & Co | 150,000 | 175,000 | 50,000 |\n| Bisco Solutions | 125,000 | 180,000 | 25,000 |\n| Casper Nig. Ltd | 85,000 | 200,000 | 40,000 |","note":"The source answer key is used here. It applies 17%, although the passage states 17.5% for unregistered firms; at 17.5% the answer would be ₦17,500, which is not an option.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-34"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"For Weeks 1 and 3, across all 5 stores combined, what was the difference (in units) between actual and target sales?","opts":["10 over target (Week 1); 10 under target (Week 3)","10 over target (Week 1); 15 under target (Week 3)","15 over target (Week 1); 10 under target (Week 3)","15 over target (Week 1); 15 under target (Week 3)","20 over target (Week 1); 10 under target (Week 3)"],"ans":4,"exp":"Week 1: actual 20+30+25+15+5 = 95 vs target 15+20+20+10+10 = 75 → 20 over. Week 3: actual 35+40+24+12+9 = 120 vs target 35+35+30+15+15 = 130 → 10 under.","passage":"The table shows weekly units sold (actual and target) and the number of sales staff at five Western Region stores.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_stores.png","imgLabel":"Western Region store sales","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-35"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Over the three-week period, which store achieved the highest sales per sales staff member?","opts":["Redcliff","Ather","Wilkington","Trew","Tunston"],"ans":2,"exp":"Total units ÷ staff: Redcliff 75/8 = 9.4; Ather 110/9 = 12.2; Wilkington 67/5 = 13.4; Trew 41/8 = 5.1; Tunston 20/6 = 3.3. Wilkington is highest.","passage":"The table shows weekly units sold (actual and target) and the number of sales staff at five Western Region stores.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_stores.png","imgLabel":"Western Region store sales","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-36"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Next year staff numbers will be cut by 1 at stores with 6 or fewer staff and by 2 at all other stores. What will be the average monthly target per staff member across all 5 stores if the regional monthly target is £168,000?","opts":["£5,000","£6,000","£7,000","£8,000","£9,000"],"ans":1,"exp":"New staff: Redcliff 6, Ather 7, Wilkington 4, Trew 6, Tunston 5 = 28. £168,000 ÷ 28 = £6,000.","passage":"The table shows weekly units sold (actual and target) and the number of sales staff at five Western Region stores.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_stores.png","imgLabel":"Western Region store sales","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-37"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"The Western Region’s overall sales (£120,000) were in a ratio of 3:2 to the Eastern Region’s sales, which were half the sales of the Northern and Southern Regions combined. What were the total sales of all 4 regions?","opts":["£180,000","£200,000","£220,000","£240,000","£360,000"],"ans":4,"exp":"Eastern = 120,000 × 2/3 = £80,000. Northern + Southern = 2 × 80,000 = £160,000. Total = 120,000 + 80,000 + 160,000 = £360,000.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-38"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"All sales in the three-week period were based on an average £9.50 reduction in the sales price. What was the total saving made by customers over the 3 weeks (to the nearest £100)?","opts":["£3,000","£3,500","£4,000","£4,500","£5,000"],"ans":0,"exp":"Units sold: Week 1 = 95, Week 2 = 20+40+18+14+6 = 98, Week 3 = 120 → 313 units. 313 × £9.50 = £2,973.50 ≈ £3,000.","passage":"The table shows weekly units sold (actual and target) and the number of sales staff at five Western Region stores.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_stores.png","imgLabel":"Western Region store sales","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-39"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"What was the unit-sales ratio of Austria Quarter 4 : Portugal Quarter 1 : Greece Quarter 4?","opts":["35:28:22","5:3:4","6:4:3","5:4:3","3:4:2"],"ans":3,"exp":"35,000 : 28,000 : 21,000 — divide by 7,000 → 5 : 4 : 3.","passage":"The table shows quarterly unit sales, the annual unit-sales target and the annual product-sales target (€) for five countries.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_demographic.png","imgLabel":"Total product sales by demographic","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-40"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Which country met or exceeded its annual target for unit sales?","opts":["Greece","Portugal","Austria","Ireland","Croatia"],"ans":2,"exp":"Annual totals vs targets: Greece 108,300 (110,000); Portugal 104,200 (105,000); Austria 105,800 (105,000) ✓; Ireland 102,400 (105,000); Croatia 105,200 (110,000). Only Austria.","passage":"The table shows quarterly unit sales, the annual unit-sales target and the annual product-sales target (€) for five countries.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_demographic.png","imgLabel":"Total product sales by demographic","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-41"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"The previous year’s average number of Portuguese units sold per quarter was 20% higher than in the year shown. What was that previous-year quarterly average?","opts":["104,200","31,260","26,050","21,260","20,840"],"ans":1,"exp":"This year: (28,000 + 33,200 + 22,600 + 20,400) ÷ 4 = 26,050 per quarter. Previous year = 26,050 × 1.2 = 31,260.","passage":"The table shows quarterly unit sales, the annual unit-sales target and the annual product-sales target (€) for five countries.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_demographic.png","imgLabel":"Total product sales by demographic","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-42"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If Austria’s annual corporation tax was 22% on the first 200,000 of sales and 20% on sales above 200,000, what is its tax bill for the year (each unit sells at 3.5)?","opts":["34,000","34,060","37,060","44,000","78,060"],"ans":4,"exp":"Sales value = 105,800 units × 3.5 = 370,300. Tax = 22% × 200,000 + 20% × 170,300 = 44,000 + 34,060 = 78,060.","passage":"The table shows quarterly unit sales, the annual unit-sales target and the annual product-sales target (€) for five countries.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_demographic.png","imgLabel":"Total product sales by demographic","note":"The source listed 78,000 as option E but calculated 78,060; the option now shows the correct value.","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-43"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Greek and Irish sales generated €2.50 per unit sold; the other countries generated €2.25 per unit. Which country or countries exceeded their annual product-sales target?","opts":["Portugal and Austria","Ireland and Austria","Croatia and Austria","Croatia and Greece","Ireland and Greece"],"ans":3,"exp":"Greece 108,300 × 2.5 = 270,750 (> 250,000 ✓); Portugal 234,450 (< 240,000); Austria 238,050 (< 240,000); Ireland 256,000 (< 260,000); Croatia 105,200 × 2.25 = 236,700 (> 230,000 ✓).","passage":"The table shows quarterly unit sales, the annual unit-sales target and the annual product-sales target (€) for five countries.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_demographic.png","imgLabel":"Total product sales by demographic","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-44"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If worldwide sales consist of US, EU and Far Eastern sales, which product had the highest annual Far Eastern sales?","opts":["Product A","Product B","Product C","Product D","Product E"],"ans":0,"exp":"Far East = worldwide − (US + EU annual): A 320 − (105 + 181.9) = 33.1; B 300 − 270.3 = 29.7; C 330 − 298.7 = 31.3; D 380 − 354.1 = 25.9; E 350 − 334.9 = 15.1. Product A is highest.","passage":"The table shows half-yearly US and EU sales (£ millions), annual sales targets and worldwide sales for five products, with the US and EU sales-tax rules.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_products.png","imgLabel":"Product sales (£ millions)","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-45"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"For the five products combined, how did the gap between total annual sales and the total annual sales target compare for the US and the EU?","opts":["£27.1 million (US); £25.8 million (EU)","£638.3 million (US); £908.2 million (EU)","£4.7 million (US); £10.4 million (EU)","£271.7 million (US); £258.2 million (EU)","Cannot tell"],"ans":2,"exp":"US sales 325.3 + 320 = 645.3 vs target 650 → £4.7m short. EU sales 445.2 + 454.4 = 899.6 vs target 910 → £10.4m short.","passage":"The table shows half-yearly US and EU sales (£ millions), annual sales targets and worldwide sales for five products, with the US and EU sales-tax rules.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_products.png","imgLabel":"Product sales (£ millions)","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-46"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If annual EU sales of Products B and C are both split online : offline in the ratio 2:3, what are the combined online EU sales of B and C?","opts":["£198,120,000","£19,812,000","£13,208,000","£132,080,000","None of these"],"ans":3,"exp":"EU annual sales: B 72.2 + 77.8 = 150; C 88 + 92.2 = 180.2; total £330.2m. Online share = 2/5 → 330.2 × 0.4 = £132.08m = £132,080,000.","passage":"The table shows half-yearly US and EU sales (£ millions), annual sales targets and worldwide sales for five products, with the US and EU sales-tax rules.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_products.png","imgLabel":"Product sales (£ millions)","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-47"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"How much US and EU annual sales tax is due for Products B, C and D combined (to the nearest £million)?","opts":["£244 million","£211 million","£149 million","£243 million","£120 million"],"ans":2,"exp":"US: combined B+C+D annual sales = 120.3 + 118.5 + 150.6 = 389.4, so tax = 24% × first £130m = £31.2m. EU: 150 + 180.2 + 203.5 = 533.7 × 22% = £117.4m. Total ≈ £148.6m ≈ £149m.","passage":"The table shows half-yearly US and EU sales (£ millions), annual sales targets and worldwide sales for five products, with the US and EU sales-tax rules.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_products.png","imgLabel":"Product sales (£ millions)","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-48"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Which of the following represents the smallest amount?","opts":["Product B’s change in EU sales between Jan–June and July–Dec","7% of Product D’s US sales (Jan–June)","Product E’s change in US sales between Jan–June and July–Dec","Average US Product A sales per month (July–Dec)","Average US Product C sales per month (Jan–June)"],"ans":1,"exp":"A: 77.8 − 72.2 = 5.6. B: 7% × 76.5 = 5.355. C: 78.2 − 72.7 = 5.5. D: 50.5 ÷ 6 = 8.42. E: 60.5 ÷ 6 = 10.08. The smallest is B (£5.355m).","passage":"The table shows half-yearly US and EU sales (£ millions), annual sales targets and worldwide sales for five products, with the US and EU sales-tax rules.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf1_products.png","imgLabel":"Product sales (£ millions)","src":"Workforce Test 1.0","id":"workforce-49"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Flexible working arrangements are conducive to better job satisfaction for employees.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — employees able to work flexibly are “happier at work, experiencing greater job satisfaction”.","passage":"Many businesses now recognise that there are wide-ranging benefits to be gained from offering their workforce flexible working arrangements. These arrangements can include being able to work from home, job sharing, part-time working and being able to choose when to complete agreed hours, for example through working fewer but longer days, or working flexitime. Employees able to adopt flexible working are better able to balance work and family commitments and are therefore happier at work, experiencing greater job satisfaction. They are also more motivated and less likely to leave, which is important because staff recruitment costs are not insignificant to businesses. Businesses reap the benefits through productivity increases and greater cost-effectiveness. Customers can benefit too as businesses are better able to react quickly to changes in the market. All employees have a statutory right to request flexible working arrangements; the law extends no further than insisting employers consider and respond to these requests within a predefined timescale.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-50"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Employees are legally entitled to work flexible hours if they request to do so.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"False — employees only have a right to request flexible working; the law merely requires employers to consider and respond to requests, not to grant them.","passage":"Many businesses now recognise that there are wide-ranging benefits to be gained from offering their workforce flexible working arrangements. These arrangements can include being able to work from home, job sharing, part-time working and being able to choose when to complete agreed hours, for example through working fewer but longer days, or working flexitime. Employees able to adopt flexible working are better able to balance work and family commitments and are therefore happier at work, experiencing greater job satisfaction. They are also more motivated and less likely to leave, which is important because staff recruitment costs are not insignificant to businesses. Businesses reap the benefits through productivity increases and greater cost-effectiveness. Customers can benefit too as businesses are better able to react quickly to changes in the market. All employees have a statutory right to request flexible working arrangements; the law extends no further than insisting employers consider and respond to these requests within a predefined timescale.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-51"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Employers may save on recruitment costs if they introduce flexible working hours.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — flexible workers are “less likely to leave”, which matters because recruitment costs “are not insignificant”.","passage":"Many businesses now recognise that there are wide-ranging benefits to be gained from offering their workforce flexible working arrangements. These arrangements can include being able to work from home, job sharing, part-time working and being able to choose when to complete agreed hours, for example through working fewer but longer days, or working flexitime. Employees able to adopt flexible working are better able to balance work and family commitments and are therefore happier at work, experiencing greater job satisfaction. They are also more motivated and less likely to leave, which is important because staff recruitment costs are not insignificant to businesses. Businesses reap the benefits through productivity increases and greater cost-effectiveness. Customers can benefit too as businesses are better able to react quickly to changes in the market. All employees have a statutory right to request flexible working arrangements; the law extends no further than insisting employers consider and respond to these requests within a predefined timescale.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-52"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"People who think their boss is incompetent are twenty-five percent more likely to suffer stress.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — the 25% figure in the study refers to the risk of serious heart disease, not stress, and it applies to the 3,000 men studied, not people in general.","passage":"In a new study of 3,000 employed men in the 19–70 age range over a period of 10 years, researchers found that those working for inconsiderate bosses were more likely to suffer from stress and stress-related illness than those working for more considerate and enlightened bosses. Previous research had shown that employees with inconsiderate bosses suffered from high blood pressure, which is known to increase the risk of heart disease. In this latest survey the researchers asked employees to assess the competence of their bosses as leaders. They found that those who had categorised their boss as incompetent were 25% more at risk of serious heart disease and that this level of risk increased the longer they continued to work for the same boss. The statistics took into account external factors such as social class, educational background, lifestyle and any existing illness. The researchers recommended that, should their findings be confirmed, managers should be given training to help them improve their working relationships.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-53"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"This latest study is the first to suggest a link between the behaviour of bosses and the blood pressure of their employees.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"False — “Previous research had shown that employees with inconsiderate bosses suffered from high blood pressure.”","passage":"In a new study of 3,000 employed men in the 19–70 age range over a period of 10 years, researchers found that those working for inconsiderate bosses were more likely to suffer from stress and stress-related illness than those working for more considerate and enlightened bosses. Previous research had shown that employees with inconsiderate bosses suffered from high blood pressure, which is known to increase the risk of heart disease. In this latest survey the researchers asked employees to assess the competence of their bosses as leaders. They found that those who had categorised their boss as incompetent were 25% more at risk of serious heart disease and that this level of risk increased the longer they continued to work for the same boss. The statistics took into account external factors such as social class, educational background, lifestyle and any existing illness. The researchers recommended that, should their findings be confirmed, managers should be given training to help them improve their working relationships.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-54"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Managers should be given training to help them improve their working relationships.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — this is only the researchers’ recommendation, and it is conditional on the findings being confirmed.","passage":"In a new study of 3,000 employed men in the 19–70 age range over a period of 10 years, researchers found that those working for inconsiderate bosses were more likely to suffer from stress and stress-related illness than those working for more considerate and enlightened bosses. Previous research had shown that employees with inconsiderate bosses suffered from high blood pressure, which is known to increase the risk of heart disease. In this latest survey the researchers asked employees to assess the competence of their bosses as leaders. They found that those who had categorised their boss as incompetent were 25% more at risk of serious heart disease and that this level of risk increased the longer they continued to work for the same boss. The statistics took into account external factors such as social class, educational background, lifestyle and any existing illness. The researchers recommended that, should their findings be confirmed, managers should be given training to help them improve their working relationships.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-55"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Leading by example is an effective approach in dealing with problematic behaviour from employees.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — when a team member’s behaviour conflicts with desirable practice, “one effective angle is to lead by example”.","passage":"A common difficulty faced by business managers is when the behaviour of a team member conflicts with established desirable practice. How does a good leader handle such an issue? One effective angle is to lead by example: instead of waiting for a problem to develop, take a proactive approach in heading it off with reference to clearly laid-out guidelines. If a problematic situation does develop, controlling it can be made simpler by invoking existing values from a mission statement which has been set out in advance. A good team will always put the needs of the organisation first.\n\nTaking such an approach gives the team a sense of personal involvement which encourages them to feel part of the organisation’s mission, internalising the needs of the group rather than feeling a sense of externally imposed obligation. It provides team members with a greater sense of personal control, the sense that they have contributed individually, and by choice, to the well-being of their organisation.\n\nTo achieve this, a manager must have a good understanding of the way individual people communicate — a flexible approach is essential, using real-life practical examples relevant to each team member’s particular experience.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-56"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"A manager who understands how people communicate is able to take a flexible approach in dealing with problems.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — the passage says both are needed but never says that understanding communication leads to a flexible approach.","passage":"A common difficulty faced by business managers is when the behaviour of a team member conflicts with established desirable practice. How does a good leader handle such an issue? One effective angle is to lead by example: instead of waiting for a problem to develop, take a proactive approach in heading it off with reference to clearly laid-out guidelines. If a problematic situation does develop, controlling it can be made simpler by invoking existing values from a mission statement which has been set out in advance. A good team will always put the needs of the organisation first.\n\nTaking such an approach gives the team a sense of personal involvement which encourages them to feel part of the organisation’s mission, internalising the needs of the group rather than feeling a sense of externally imposed obligation. It provides team members with a greater sense of personal control, the sense that they have contributed individually, and by choice, to the well-being of their organisation.\n\nTo achieve this, a manager must have a good understanding of the way individual people communicate — a flexible approach is essential, using real-life practical examples relevant to each team member’s particular experience.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-57"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"In a good team, the needs of the organisation are secondary to the needs of the individual.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"False — “A good team will always put the needs of the organisation first.”","passage":"A common difficulty faced by business managers is when the behaviour of a team member conflicts with established desirable practice. How does a good leader handle such an issue? One effective angle is to lead by example: instead of waiting for a problem to develop, take a proactive approach in heading it off with reference to clearly laid-out guidelines. If a problematic situation does develop, controlling it can be made simpler by invoking existing values from a mission statement which has been set out in advance. A good team will always put the needs of the organisation first.\n\nTaking such an approach gives the team a sense of personal involvement which encourages them to feel part of the organisation’s mission, internalising the needs of the group rather than feeling a sense of externally imposed obligation. It provides team members with a greater sense of personal control, the sense that they have contributed individually, and by choice, to the well-being of their organisation.\n\nTo achieve this, a manager must have a good understanding of the way individual people communicate — a flexible approach is essential, using real-life practical examples relevant to each team member’s particular experience.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-58"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Employees instilled with the idea of personal entrepreneurship will be less willing to contribute to the success of the company as a whole.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"False — they contribute “as willing partners to the achievements of the company as a whole”.","passage":"The secret to success in business is entrepreneurial spirit at all levels of the company. Employees who are identified as entrepreneurs in their own right are more motivated — their own financial success becomes integrated with the company’s. Those who are oriented towards personal entrepreneurship will work long hours to develop their own tried-and-tested business practices and strategies, contributing as willing partners to the achievements of the company as a whole.\n\nOrientation and value-formation training can induce this kind of thinking in new staff recruits, inculcating the notion of how quickly it is possible to achieve financial security through hard work and innovative business approaches, combined with the impression that to miss out on opportunities for such rapid economic and social advancement would be at best unwise and at worst catastrophic.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-59"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"New staff members can be indoctrinated with the virtues of entrepreneurship.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — orientation and value-formation training “can induce this kind of thinking in new staff recruits”.","passage":"The secret to success in business is entrepreneurial spirit at all levels of the company. Employees who are identified as entrepreneurs in their own right are more motivated — their own financial success becomes integrated with the company’s. Those who are oriented towards personal entrepreneurship will work long hours to develop their own tried-and-tested business practices and strategies, contributing as willing partners to the achievements of the company as a whole.\n\nOrientation and value-formation training can induce this kind of thinking in new staff recruits, inculcating the notion of how quickly it is possible to achieve financial security through hard work and innovative business approaches, combined with the impression that to miss out on opportunities for such rapid economic and social advancement would be at best unwise and at worst catastrophic.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-60"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Employees encouraged to think of themselves as entrepreneurs work fewer hours than other staff members.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — they “work long hours”, but the passage makes no comparison with other staff.","passage":"The secret to success in business is entrepreneurial spirit at all levels of the company. Employees who are identified as entrepreneurs in their own right are more motivated — their own financial success becomes integrated with the company’s. Those who are oriented towards personal entrepreneurship will work long hours to develop their own tried-and-tested business practices and strategies, contributing as willing partners to the achievements of the company as a whole.\n\nOrientation and value-formation training can induce this kind of thinking in new staff recruits, inculcating the notion of how quickly it is possible to achieve financial security through hard work and innovative business approaches, combined with the impression that to miss out on opportunities for such rapid economic and social advancement would be at best unwise and at worst catastrophic.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-61"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"There is no legislation covering discrimination at work, so employers have to develop their own ways of preventing it.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"False — “most forms of discrimination in the workplace have been outlawed”, so legislation does exist.","passage":"While most forms of discrimination in the workplace have been outlawed, discrimination or bias against some employees seeking career advancement still happens. This discrimination is both unwritten and unacknowledged. A “glass ceiling” is the term used to describe this type of discrimination and refers to the invisible barrier that people hit when they try to progress beyond a certain level in some businesses and organisations. Originally coined to illustrate the hidden use of sexual discrimination against women in professional environments, it is now used to describe any form of discrimination, such as racism or ageism, which prevents qualified or experienced employees reaching even basic levels within their organisation. Some reports and studies now suggest that change is happening and that cracks are beginning to appear in the glass. The studies also claim, however, that change is happening slowly and that the cracks are small.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-62"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Males are less likely to experience the glass ceiling effect than females.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — the term began with discrimination against women but now covers any discrimination; no comparison of how often men and women experience it is given.","passage":"While most forms of discrimination in the workplace have been outlawed, discrimination or bias against some employees seeking career advancement still happens. This discrimination is both unwritten and unacknowledged. A “glass ceiling” is the term used to describe this type of discrimination and refers to the invisible barrier that people hit when they try to progress beyond a certain level in some businesses and organisations. Originally coined to illustrate the hidden use of sexual discrimination against women in professional environments, it is now used to describe any form of discrimination, such as racism or ageism, which prevents qualified or experienced employees reaching even basic levels within their organisation. Some reports and studies now suggest that change is happening and that cracks are beginning to appear in the glass. The studies also claim, however, that change is happening slowly and that the cracks are small.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-63"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"A glass ceiling can prevent qualified people from getting to the top of their field.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — it describes discrimination that “prevents qualified or experienced employees reaching even basic levels within their organisation”.","passage":"While most forms of discrimination in the workplace have been outlawed, discrimination or bias against some employees seeking career advancement still happens. This discrimination is both unwritten and unacknowledged. A “glass ceiling” is the term used to describe this type of discrimination and refers to the invisible barrier that people hit when they try to progress beyond a certain level in some businesses and organisations. Originally coined to illustrate the hidden use of sexual discrimination against women in professional environments, it is now used to describe any form of discrimination, such as racism or ageism, which prevents qualified or experienced employees reaching even basic levels within their organisation. Some reports and studies now suggest that change is happening and that cracks are beginning to appear in the glass. The studies also claim, however, that change is happening slowly and that the cracks are small.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-64"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Which of the following best completes the passage below?\n\nIn a survey of job applicants, two-fifths admitted to being at least a little dishonest. However, the survey may underestimate the proportion of job applicants who are dishonest, because ____.","opts":["some dishonest people taking the survey might have claimed on the survey to be honest","some generally honest people taking the survey might have claimed on the survey to be dishonest","some people who claimed on the survey to be at least a little dishonest may be very dishonest","some people who claimed on the survey to be dishonest may have been answering honestly","some people who are not job applicants are probably at least a little dishonest"],"ans":0,"exp":"If dishonest applicants claimed to be honest, the survey would undercount dishonesty — an underestimate (A). B would cause an overestimate; C is about degrees of dishonesty; D does not produce an underestimate; E concerns non-applicants.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-65"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Samuel is obviously a bad fisherman. During the past season, in which he and the five members of his team spent four months on a boat together off Dutch Harbor, AK, he caught fewer fish than any of his teammates.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, most weakens the argument that Samuel is a bad fisherman?","opts":["Two seasons ago, Samuel fished on another boat off Dutch Harbor and caught more fish than any other member of that boat.","Before becoming a fisherman, Samuel piloted a fishing boat whose members regularly caught record numbers of fish.","While fishing this past season, Samuel fell sick for a week and did not catch any fish during this time.","Unlike the other fishermen on his boat, at the order of the captain, Samuel fished this past season with experimental bait.","Amongst the fishing community in Dutch Harbor, Samuel has a reputation for being an especially bad fisherman."],"ans":3,"exp":"D gives a reason outside Samuel’s skill — and not his own choice — that uniquely handicapped him this season, so his low catch does not show he is a bad fisherman. A is an old season; B concerns piloting, not fishing; C (one week) is too small to explain four months; E supports the argument.","note":"The source printed an unrelated question stem (“minimize Company X’s losses on the policies”); it has been replaced with the stem that matches the passage and options.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-66"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Eating beets significantly lowers the risk of cancer, according to an article in a nutritional magazine. The article refers to a study that found that people who consumed one or more beets per day were half as likely to be diagnosed with the disease as people who did not.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, most weakens the argument in the magazine article?","opts":["Another study found that people who consumed one tablespoon of flax seed oil per day were more than four times less likely to be diagnosed with cancer as those who did not.","Participants in the study reported consuming no vegetables other than beets.","The study was only conducted in one city.","In another experiment, cancer patients who ate one or more beets per day were no more likely to recover than those who ate no beets.","The participants in the study who ate beets were more likely to exercise regularly than those who did not eat beets."],"ans":4,"exp":"E introduces a confounding factor: the beet-eaters also exercised more, so exercise rather than beets could explain the lower cancer rate. A is off-topic, B strengthens the argument, C is a minor limitation, and D concerns recovery rather than prevention.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-67"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"The cost of producing radios in Country Q is ten percent less than the cost of producing radios in Country Y. Even after transportation fees and tariff charges are added, it is still cheaper for a company to import radios from Country Q to Country Y than to produce radios in Country Y.\n\nThe statements above, if true, best support which of the following assertions?","opts":["Labor costs in Country Q are ten percent below those in Country Y.","Importing radios from Country Q to Country Y will eliminate ten percent of the manufacturing jobs in Country Y.","The tariff on a radio imported from Country Q to Country Y is less than ten percent of the cost of manufacturing the radio in Country Y.","The fee for transporting a radio from Country Q to Country Y is more than ten percent of the cost of manufacturing the radio in Country Q.","It takes ten percent less time to manufacture a radio in Country Q than it does in Country Y."],"ans":2,"exp":"Importing stays cheaper even after transport and tariff are added, so those extra costs together must be less than the 10% saving — which means the tariff alone must be less than 10% of Y’s production cost (C). A and E are possible causes but unsupported; B and D are not implied.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-68"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Toughened hiring standards have not been the primary cause of the present staffing shortage in public schools. The shortage of teachers is primarily caused by the fact that in recent years teachers have not experienced any improvements in working conditions and their salaries have not kept pace with salaries in other professions.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, would most support the claims above?","opts":["Many teachers already in the profession would not have been hired under the new hiring standards.","Today more teachers are entering the profession with a higher educational level than in the past.","Some teachers have cited higher standards for hiring as a reason for the current staffing shortage.","Many teachers have cited low pay and lack of professional freedom as reasons for their leaving the profession.","Many prospective teachers have cited the new hiring standards as a reason for not entering the profession."],"ans":3,"exp":"D directly backs the claimed cause (pay and conditions) by showing teachers leave for those reasons. A, C and E point to hiring standards — the explanation being rejected — and B does not discriminate between the two explanations.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-69"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":1,"exp":"Rule 1: the top-row shapes follow the order circle, circle, square, square, repeating. Rule 2: the top-row shapes move one place right each time. Rule 3: the bottom-row shape alternates between circle and square. That gives B.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_q21.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-70"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":4,"exp":"Rule 1: the solid line moves one place anticlockwise each time. Rule 2: the arrowed line moves two places anticlockwise. Rule 3: the dotted line moves anticlockwise one place, then two, then three, and so on. That gives E.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_q22.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-71"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":2,"exp":"Rule 1: the left and right rectangles alternate between pointing NE–SW and NW–SE. Rule 2: the middle rectangle alternates between foreground and background. Rule 3: the shaded block moves one place right each time. That gives C.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_q23.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-72"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":1,"exp":"Rule 1: the number of edges on the shape decreases by one each time. Rule 2: the number of segments inside it increases by one. Rule 3: the shape alternates between shaded and unshaded. That gives B.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_q24.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-73"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":3,"exp":"Rule 1: the number of circles increases by one each time. Rule 2: the triangles alternate between shaded and unshaded. Rule 3: the number of squares alternates between 5 and 7. That gives D.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_q25.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-74"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":0,"exp":"Rule 1: the shaded bar moves one place right, then two, then three. Rule 2: the rounded corners move one place right each time. Rule 3: the shortened bar alternates between left and right. That gives A.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_q26.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-75"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":0,"exp":"Rule 1: the missing edge of the square moves one place anticlockwise. Rule 2: the number moves one place anticlockwise and shows how many circles are in the square. Rule 3: the square alternates between shaded and unshaded. That gives A.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_q27.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-76"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":4,"exp":"Rule 1: the large shape alternates between top and bottom of the box. Rule 2: the large shape matches whichever small shape is most frequent. Rule 3: if more small shapes are shaded than unshaded, the large shape is shaded too, and vice versa. That gives E.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_q28.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-77"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":1,"exp":"Rule 1: the large shape alternates between circle and square. Rule 2: the small shape gains one edge each time. Rule 3: the line rotates one place clockwise each time. That gives B.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_q29.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-78"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":0,"exp":"Rule 1: vertical lines increase by one every two boxes. Rule 2: horizontal lines increase by one each time. Rule 3: shaded boxes increase by two each time. That gives A.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_q30.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-79"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"After adding his profit to a bottle of wine, Frank’s pre-V.A.T price came to £72.00. What should Frank put as the selling price including V.A.T?","opts":["£82.80","£85.60","£88.60","£89.20","£89.70"],"ans":0,"exp":"Selling price = pre-V.A.T price × 1.15 = £72.00 × 1.15 = £82.80.","passage":"In the United Kingdom, shops include V.A.T (Value Added Tax) in the price of the goods they sell, so the V.A.T is paid by the purchaser. The current normal rate of V.A.T is 15% of the pre-V.A.T price.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-80"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Charles sold a cane rack for £90.00 including V.A.T. What was its price before V.A.T was added?","opts":["£67.20","£77.20","£78.20","£88.20","£89.20"],"ans":2,"exp":"Pre-V.A.T price = £90.00 ÷ 1.15 = £78.26 ≈ £78.20 (the closest option).","passage":"In the United Kingdom, shops include V.A.T (Value Added Tax) in the price of the goods they sell, so the V.A.T is paid by the purchaser. The current normal rate of V.A.T is 15% of the pre-V.A.T price.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-81"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"What is the V.A.T amount on a product whose pre-V.A.T price is £84.00?","opts":["£10.50","£12.60","£14.70","£15.80","£16.90"],"ans":1,"exp":"V.A.T = 15% × £84.00 = £12.60.","passage":"In the United Kingdom, shops include V.A.T (Value Added Tax) in the price of the goods they sell, so the V.A.T is paid by the purchaser. The current normal rate of V.A.T is 15% of the pre-V.A.T price.","note":"Reworded from “a product sold for £84.00”, which would include V.A.T and give £10.96 — not an option. The intended working is 15% of £84.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-82"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"A pair of trousers should cost £50.00 before V.A.T. Mary passes the V.A.T on to the buyer. What price does she put on them?","opts":["£43.10","£45.50","£55.70","£57.50","£61.10"],"ans":3,"exp":"Price including V.A.T = £50.00 × 1.15 = £57.50.","passage":"In the United Kingdom, shops include V.A.T (Value Added Tax) in the price of the goods they sell, so the V.A.T is paid by the purchaser. The current normal rate of V.A.T is 15% of the pre-V.A.T price.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-83"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Goods are tagged for sale at £120 including V.A.T. What was their price before V.A.T?","opts":["£104.35","£106.20","£106.40","£108.30","£108.45"],"ans":0,"exp":"Pre-V.A.T price = £120 ÷ 1.15 = £104.35.","passage":"In the United Kingdom, shops include V.A.T (Value Added Tax) in the price of the goods they sell, so the V.A.T is paid by the purchaser. The current normal rate of V.A.T is 15% of the pre-V.A.T price.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-84"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If next year’s projections prove accurate, which region will have maintained or increased its sales every year from the previous year through to next year?","opts":["Northern Region","Southern Region","Eastern Region","Western Region","Central Region"],"ans":0,"exp":"Northern: 310 → 310 → 320 (maintained, then increased). Southern falls to 160; Eastern falls to 275 next year; Western falls to 270; Central falls to 90.","passage":"The table shows total sales (£ millions) by region for the previous year, the current year and next year’s projection.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_regions.png","imgLabel":"Total sales by region","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-85"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"What is the difference between the highest- and lowest-performing regions in the current year (to the nearest £million)?","opts":["£216 million","£217 million","£218 million","£219 million","£220 million"],"ans":4,"exp":"Highest = Northern £310m; lowest = Central £90m. Difference = £220m.","passage":"The table shows total sales (£ millions) by region for the previous year, the current year and next year’s projection.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_regions.png","imgLabel":"Total sales by region","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-86"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If next year’s forecasts are scaled back by a quarter for the Northern and Western regions and by a fifth for the Southern and Eastern regions, what will total projected sales be for all 5 regions?","opts":["£1,155 million","£924 million","£919.50 million","£942 million","£866.25 million"],"ans":2,"exp":"Northern 320 × ¾ = 240; Southern 165 × ⅘ = 132; Eastern 275 × ⅘ = 220; Western 270 × ¾ = 202.5; Central 125. Total = £919.5m.","passage":"The table shows total sales (£ millions) by region for the previous year, the current year and next year’s projection.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_regions.png","imgLabel":"Total sales by region","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-87"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"What were the Central : Eastern sales ratios for the previous year and the current year?","opts":["9:30 (previous); 3:11 (current)","20:50 (previous); 3:11 (current)","10:30 (previous); 5:11 (current)","11:29 (previous); 3:10 (current)","5:11 (previous); 11:29 (current)"],"ans":3,"exp":"Previous year 110 : 290 = 11 : 29. Current year 90 : 300 = 3 : 10.","passage":"The table shows total sales (£ millions) by region for the previous year, the current year and next year’s projection.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_regions.png","imgLabel":"Total sales by region","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-88"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Put the regions in increasing order of combined sales for the current year plus next year’s projection.","opts":["Central, Southern, Western, Eastern, Northern","Central, Southern, Eastern, Western, Northern","Southern, Central, Western, Eastern, Northern","Central, Western, Southern, Eastern, Northern","Central, Southern, Western, Northern, Eastern"],"ans":0,"exp":"Current + next: Central 90 + 125 = 215; Southern 160 + 165 = 325; Western 280 + 270 = 550; Eastern 300 + 275 = 575; Northern 310 + 320 = 630.","passage":"The table shows total sales (£ millions) by region for the previous year, the current year and next year’s projection.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_regions.png","imgLabel":"Total sales by region","note":"The source options were a list of years that did not match the question; the options have been rebuilt from the worked answer.","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-89"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If profit before tax rises by 15% for Competitor B and falls by 8% for Competitor A, what is the difference between their corporation tax payments (to the nearest £million)?","opts":["£10 million","£12 million","£14 million","£16 million","£18 million"],"ans":2,"exp":"B: 112 × 1.15 = 128.8. A: 90 × 0.92 = 82.8. Difference in tax = (128.8 − 82.8) × 30% = 13.8 ≈ £14m.","passage":"The table shows consolidated income statements (£ millions) for three competitors. Corporation tax is 30%.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_competitors.png","imgLabel":"Consolidated income statements","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-90"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Competitor B and Competitor C declare their revenues in dollars and euros respectively. What are the figures? (£1 = $1.66; £1 = €1.15)","opts":["$1,043 million (B); €708 million (C)","$1,049 million (B); €690 million (C)","$1,049 million (B); €720 million (C)","$720 million (B); €1,055 million (C)","Can’t tell from the data provided"],"ans":1,"exp":"B: £632m × 1.66 = $1,049m. C: £600m × 1.15 = €690m.","passage":"The table shows consolidated income statements (£ millions) for three competitors. Corporation tax is 30%.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_competitors.png","imgLabel":"Consolidated income statements","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-91"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"What would be the difference in euros if Competitor A used an exchange rate of £1 = €1.20 rather than £1 = €1.15 to convert its profit after tax?","opts":["€0.05 million","€1.15 million","€2.05 million","€3.05 million","€3.15 million"],"ans":4,"exp":"Profit after tax = £63m. Difference = 63 × (1.20 − 1.15) = 63 × 0.05 = €3.15m.","passage":"The table shows consolidated income statements (£ millions) for three competitors. Corporation tax is 30%.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_competitors.png","imgLabel":"Consolidated income statements","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-92"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"What was the average gross profit across the 3 competitors (to the nearest £10 million)?","opts":["£140 million","£141 million","£142 million","£143 million","£144 million"],"ans":0,"exp":"(128 + 148 + 147) ÷ 3 = 423 ÷ 3 = £141m, which is £140m to the nearest £10 million.","passage":"The table shows consolidated income statements (£ millions) for three competitors. Corporation tax is 30%.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_competitors.png","imgLabel":"Consolidated income statements","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-93"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Competitor C moves to a country charging 15% corporation tax, and corporation tax falls to 22% for Competitors A and B. What is the total corporation tax payable by the 3 competitors (based on the profit before tax shown)?","opts":["£62 million","£46 million","£26 million","£25 million","Can’t tell from the data"],"ans":0,"exp":"A: 90 × 22% = 19.8. B: 112 × 22% = 24.64. C: 117 × 15% = 17.55. Total ≈ £62m.","passage":"The table shows consolidated income statements (£ millions) for three competitors. Corporation tax is 30%.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_competitors.png","imgLabel":"Consolidated income statements","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-94"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"From 2006 to 2007 attributable profit grew at double the percentage rate it grew between 2005 and 2006. What was attributable profit in 2005?","opts":["£0.23 million","£1.03 million","£1.83 million","£1.87 million","£2.03 million"],"ans":3,"exp":"2006→2007 growth = 2.11 ÷ 1.95 − 1 = 8.2%. So 2005→2006 growth = 4.1%. 2005 profit = 1.95 ÷ 1.041 ≈ £1.87m.","passage":"The table shows adjusted earnings, cash flow, attributable profit (£ millions), average profit per 500 units and average sales price per unit for 2006–2010.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_earnings.png","imgLabel":"Financial summary 2006–2010","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-95"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If the target was an average profit per unit in excess of 50p, in which year or years was it achieved?","opts":["2006","2006 & 2007","2010","2007 and 2010","2006, 2007 & 2010"],"ans":3,"exp":"Profit per unit = profit per 500 units ÷ 500: 2006 £0.50; 2007 £0.65; 2008 £0.35; 2009 £0.40; 2010 £0.60. “In excess of” 50p excludes 2006, so 2007 and 2010.","passage":"The table shows adjusted earnings, cash flow, attributable profit (£ millions), average profit per 500 units and average sales price per unit for 2006–2010.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_earnings.png","imgLabel":"Financial summary 2006–2010","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-96"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"In 2011, adjusted earnings rise by an eighth, and 2011 adjusted earnings : 2011 cash flow = 2 : 3. What will 2011 cash flow be?","opts":["£2.14 million","£1.87 million","£1.25 million","£0.83 million","£0.14 million"],"ans":1,"exp":"2011 adjusted earnings = 1.11 × 9/8 = 1.249. Cash flow = 1.249 × 3/2 ≈ £1.87m.","passage":"The table shows adjusted earnings, cash flow, attributable profit (£ millions), average profit per 500 units and average sales price per unit for 2006–2010.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_earnings.png","imgLabel":"Financial summary 2006–2010","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-97"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Which year had the lowest ratio of adjusted earnings to attributable profit?","opts":["2006","2007","2008","2009","2010"],"ans":2,"exp":"Ratios: 2006 1.02/1.95 = 0.523; 2007 1.05/2.11 = 0.498; 2008 0.95/1.93 = 0.492; 2009 0.98/1.88 = 0.521; 2010 1.11/2.23 = 0.498. Lowest is 2008.","passage":"The table shows adjusted earnings, cash flow, attributable profit (£ millions), average profit per 500 units and average sales price per unit for 2006–2010.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_earnings.png","imgLabel":"Financial summary 2006–2010","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-98"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Which year from 2007 onwards showed the greatest percentage change in cash flow compared with the preceding year?","opts":["2007","2008","2009","2010","All equal"],"ans":3,"exp":"Cash-flow changes: 2007 +7.6%; 2008 −5.6%; 2009 −6.7%; 2010 (1.25 → 1.53) +22.4%. The biggest change is 2010.","passage":"The table shows adjusted earnings, cash flow, attributable profit (£ millions), average profit per 500 units and average sales price per unit for 2006–2010.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf2_earnings.png","imgLabel":"Financial summary 2006–2010","src":"Workforce Test 2.0","id":"workforce-99"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Employees must reject criticism to build a good working relationship with their boss.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"False — the passage says the opposite: “accepting criticism gracefully” helps build a solid working relationship.","passage":"For ambitious employees, a good relationship with their immediate boss is crucial. A bad relationship can lead to missed opportunities for promotion, and even damage professional reputations. A boss who possesses a thorough understanding of the company’s future direction and ultimate goals is the person best equipped to help an employee achieve success.\n\nCommunication is key. It is important to understand a boss’s personal goals and priorities within the company, as well as their individual management approach. Clarifying instructions, anticipating needs, requesting feedback, and accepting criticism gracefully all help to build a solid working relationship.\n\nOn the other hand, artificial flattery or excessive deference are tactics unlikely to impress if promotion is the goal — a good employee should demonstrate the potential to be an equally effective boss.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-100"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"A bad relationship with a boss can lead to missed opportunities, but does not risk an employee’s reputation.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"False — a bad relationship can “even damage professional reputations”.","passage":"For ambitious employees, a good relationship with their immediate boss is crucial. A bad relationship can lead to missed opportunities for promotion, and even damage professional reputations. A boss who possesses a thorough understanding of the company’s future direction and ultimate goals is the person best equipped to help an employee achieve success.\n\nCommunication is key. It is important to understand a boss’s personal goals and priorities within the company, as well as their individual management approach. Clarifying instructions, anticipating needs, requesting feedback, and accepting criticism gracefully all help to build a solid working relationship.\n\nOn the other hand, artificial flattery or excessive deference are tactics unlikely to impress if promotion is the goal — a good employee should demonstrate the potential to be an equally effective boss.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-101"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Flattering the boss can be an effective approach for an employee seeking promotion.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — only artificial flattery is said to be “unlikely to impress”; nothing is said about sincere flattery.","passage":"For ambitious employees, a good relationship with their immediate boss is crucial. A bad relationship can lead to missed opportunities for promotion, and even damage professional reputations. A boss who possesses a thorough understanding of the company’s future direction and ultimate goals is the person best equipped to help an employee achieve success.\n\nCommunication is key. It is important to understand a boss’s personal goals and priorities within the company, as well as their individual management approach. Clarifying instructions, anticipating needs, requesting feedback, and accepting criticism gracefully all help to build a solid working relationship.\n\nOn the other hand, artificial flattery or excessive deference are tactics unlikely to impress if promotion is the goal — a good employee should demonstrate the potential to be an equally effective boss.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-102"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Unethical practices are only a problem if the public becomes aware of them.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — the passage describes the consequences of public exposure but says nothing about unethical practices that stay hidden.","passage":"Well-regulated, ethical practices should always be an area of primary concern for any business. In an environment where multinational conglomerates predominate, owners of small businesses may feel anonymous enough to become flexible about their code of ethics. However, the increasingly inescapable attention of the media allows an unprecedented number of individuals to access news and information with greater speed than ever before — unethical practices can become a matter of public knowledge overnight, with devastating consequences. Codes of ethical practice should apply not only to clients, but to employees, who are just as able to draw inappropriate behaviour on the part of their employers to the public’s attention. In today’s society, businesses of any size must be able to demonstrate transparency and accountability in their dealings with employees, clients, and the public alike.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-103"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Employees of a company should be subject to ethical codes of practice.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — “Codes of ethical practice should apply not only to clients, but to employees.”","passage":"Well-regulated, ethical practices should always be an area of primary concern for any business. In an environment where multinational conglomerates predominate, owners of small businesses may feel anonymous enough to become flexible about their code of ethics. However, the increasingly inescapable attention of the media allows an unprecedented number of individuals to access news and information with greater speed than ever before — unethical practices can become a matter of public knowledge overnight, with devastating consequences. Codes of ethical practice should apply not only to clients, but to employees, who are just as able to draw inappropriate behaviour on the part of their employers to the public’s attention. In today’s society, businesses of any size must be able to demonstrate transparency and accountability in their dealings with employees, clients, and the public alike.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-104"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"More people than ever before have access to information about companies’ ethical practices.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — the media lets “an unprecedented number of individuals” access news, and unethical practices “can become a matter of public knowledge overnight”.","passage":"Well-regulated, ethical practices should always be an area of primary concern for any business. In an environment where multinational conglomerates predominate, owners of small businesses may feel anonymous enough to become flexible about their code of ethics. However, the increasingly inescapable attention of the media allows an unprecedented number of individuals to access news and information with greater speed than ever before — unethical practices can become a matter of public knowledge overnight, with devastating consequences. Codes of ethical practice should apply not only to clients, but to employees, who are just as able to draw inappropriate behaviour on the part of their employers to the public’s attention. In today’s society, businesses of any size must be able to demonstrate transparency and accountability in their dealings with employees, clients, and the public alike.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-105"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"An analysis of competitors’ advertisements is helpful in laying out advertising strategies for a new business.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — “a helpful starting point… is an analysis of the advertising currently being used by competitors”.","passage":"Successful and cost-effective advertising is an important issue to consider when starting up a business. A comprehensive business plan should include details of advertising strategies, a helpful starting point for which is an analysis of the advertising currently being used by competitors in the same line of business.\n\nThe rise of the internet has provided a variety of new opportunities for advertising, of which an innovative business should take full advantage. A well-designed website should ideally combine a professional appearance with user-friendly functionality, and be widely promoted to draw as much traffic as possible. This not only increases the visibility of a company, but assures potential clients that the company has a forward-thinking, enterprising outlook, and is willing to embrace as well as exploit the latest technological developments.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-106"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"A professional and user-friendly website will attract a lot of traffic.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — the website should be “widely promoted to draw as much traffic as possible”; nothing says the design alone attracts traffic.","passage":"Successful and cost-effective advertising is an important issue to consider when starting up a business. A comprehensive business plan should include details of advertising strategies, a helpful starting point for which is an analysis of the advertising currently being used by competitors in the same line of business.\n\nThe rise of the internet has provided a variety of new opportunities for advertising, of which an innovative business should take full advantage. A well-designed website should ideally combine a professional appearance with user-friendly functionality, and be widely promoted to draw as much traffic as possible. This not only increases the visibility of a company, but assures potential clients that the company has a forward-thinking, enterprising outlook, and is willing to embrace as well as exploit the latest technological developments.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-107"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Clients prefer a company that is forward-thinking and enterprising.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — a website “assures potential clients” the company is forward-thinking, but the passage never says clients prefer such companies.","passage":"Successful and cost-effective advertising is an important issue to consider when starting up a business. A comprehensive business plan should include details of advertising strategies, a helpful starting point for which is an analysis of the advertising currently being used by competitors in the same line of business.\n\nThe rise of the internet has provided a variety of new opportunities for advertising, of which an innovative business should take full advantage. A well-designed website should ideally combine a professional appearance with user-friendly functionality, and be widely promoted to draw as much traffic as possible. This not only increases the visibility of a company, but assures potential clients that the company has a forward-thinking, enterprising outlook, and is willing to embrace as well as exploit the latest technological developments.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-108"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Stress at work is often caused by relationship problems with your partner.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — only relationship problems with colleagues are mentioned.","passage":"Work-related stress is one of the biggest causes of sick leave in the UK. If you’ve noticed you always seem to be rushing about, or miss meal breaks, take work home or don’t have enough time for relaxation, seeing your family or for exercise, then you may well find yourself under stress, especially at work. There is often no single cause of work-related stress, but it can be caused by poor working conditions, long hours, relationship problems with colleagues, or lack of job security. Stress is often the result of a combination of these factors that builds up over time. Work-related stress can result in both physical problems such as headaches, muscular tension, back or neck pain, tiredness, digestive problems and sweating; or emotional problems, such as a lower sex drive, feelings of inadequacy, irritability and lack of concentration. According to recent surveys, one in six of the UK working population said their job is very stressful, and thirty percent of men said that the demands of their job interfere with their private lives.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-109"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Work-related stress can result in tiredness and a lack of concentration.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — tiredness is listed among the physical problems and lack of concentration among the emotional problems.","passage":"Work-related stress is one of the biggest causes of sick leave in the UK. If you’ve noticed you always seem to be rushing about, or miss meal breaks, take work home or don’t have enough time for relaxation, seeing your family or for exercise, then you may well find yourself under stress, especially at work. There is often no single cause of work-related stress, but it can be caused by poor working conditions, long hours, relationship problems with colleagues, or lack of job security. Stress is often the result of a combination of these factors that builds up over time. Work-related stress can result in both physical problems such as headaches, muscular tension, back or neck pain, tiredness, digestive problems and sweating; or emotional problems, such as a lower sex drive, feelings of inadequacy, irritability and lack of concentration. According to recent surveys, one in six of the UK working population said their job is very stressful, and thirty percent of men said that the demands of their job interfere with their private lives.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-110"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"One in six working men say their job is very stressful.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Cannot say — one in six “of the UK working population” said this; no figure is given for men only.","passage":"Work-related stress is one of the biggest causes of sick leave in the UK. If you’ve noticed you always seem to be rushing about, or miss meal breaks, take work home or don’t have enough time for relaxation, seeing your family or for exercise, then you may well find yourself under stress, especially at work. There is often no single cause of work-related stress, but it can be caused by poor working conditions, long hours, relationship problems with colleagues, or lack of job security. Stress is often the result of a combination of these factors that builds up over time. Work-related stress can result in both physical problems such as headaches, muscular tension, back or neck pain, tiredness, digestive problems and sweating; or emotional problems, such as a lower sex drive, feelings of inadequacy, irritability and lack of concentration. According to recent surveys, one in six of the UK working population said their job is very stressful, and thirty percent of men said that the demands of their job interfere with their private lives.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-111"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Full-fat dairy products have no effect on a person’s levels of LDL.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"False — the saturated fats in full-fat dairy products raise levels of “bad” cholesterol (LDL).","passage":"Many people are aware that high cholesterol contributes to heart disease, but they will be surprised to learn that cholesterol performs essential functions in the human body. A lipid made in the liver and found in animal cell membranes, cholesterol is used to repair tissues and digest fat, as well as in the manufacture of hormones, bile salts, and Vitamin D. However, problems arise when an excess of cholesterol builds up on blood vessel walls, thus impeding blood flow. A person’s blood cholesterol count is comprised of two different types of cholesterol: low-density lipoprotein (LDL), sometimes called “bad” cholesterol, and high-density lipoprotein (HDL), known as “good” cholesterol. Whereas HDL carries cholesterol away from the arteries to the liver, LDL carries cholesterol from the liver to the arteries where it accumulates. Although certain foods deriving from animals, such as meat, shellfish, cheese and eggs, are high in dietary cholesterol, it is actually the consumption of the saturated fats found in red meat, full-fat dairy products, cakes and biscuits that raises levels of “bad” cholesterol. Conversely, eating polyunsaturated fats and soluble fibre can lower LDL.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-112"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Not everyone is aware of the true effect of cholesterol on one’s health.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"True — many people “will be surprised to learn that cholesterol performs essential functions in the human body”.","passage":"Many people are aware that high cholesterol contributes to heart disease, but they will be surprised to learn that cholesterol performs essential functions in the human body. A lipid made in the liver and found in animal cell membranes, cholesterol is used to repair tissues and digest fat, as well as in the manufacture of hormones, bile salts, and Vitamin D. However, problems arise when an excess of cholesterol builds up on blood vessel walls, thus impeding blood flow. A person’s blood cholesterol count is comprised of two different types of cholesterol: low-density lipoprotein (LDL), sometimes called “bad” cholesterol, and high-density lipoprotein (HDL), known as “good” cholesterol. Whereas HDL carries cholesterol away from the arteries to the liver, LDL carries cholesterol from the liver to the arteries where it accumulates. Although certain foods deriving from animals, such as meat, shellfish, cheese and eggs, are high in dietary cholesterol, it is actually the consumption of the saturated fats found in red meat, full-fat dairy products, cakes and biscuits that raises levels of “bad” cholesterol. Conversely, eating polyunsaturated fats and soluble fibre can lower LDL.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-113"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"To maximise blood flow in the arteries, HDL levels should be minimised.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"False — HDL carries cholesterol away from the arteries, so lowering HDL would work against blood flow.","passage":"Many people are aware that high cholesterol contributes to heart disease, but they will be surprised to learn that cholesterol performs essential functions in the human body. A lipid made in the liver and found in animal cell membranes, cholesterol is used to repair tissues and digest fat, as well as in the manufacture of hormones, bile salts, and Vitamin D. However, problems arise when an excess of cholesterol builds up on blood vessel walls, thus impeding blood flow. A person’s blood cholesterol count is comprised of two different types of cholesterol: low-density lipoprotein (LDL), sometimes called “bad” cholesterol, and high-density lipoprotein (HDL), known as “good” cholesterol. Whereas HDL carries cholesterol away from the arteries to the liver, LDL carries cholesterol from the liver to the arteries where it accumulates. Although certain foods deriving from animals, such as meat, shellfish, cheese and eggs, are high in dietary cholesterol, it is actually the consumption of the saturated fats found in red meat, full-fat dairy products, cakes and biscuits that raises levels of “bad” cholesterol. Conversely, eating polyunsaturated fats and soluble fibre can lower LDL.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-114"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"The ice on the front windshield of the car had formed when moisture condensed during the night. The ice melted quickly after the car was warmed up the next morning because the defrosting vent, which blows on the front windshield, was turned on full force.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, most seriously jeopardises the validity of the explanation for the speed with which the ice melted?","opts":["The side windows had no ice condensation on them.","Even though no attempt was made to defrost the back window, the ice there melted at the same rate as did the ice on the front windshield.","The speed at which ice on a window melts increases as the temperature of the air blown on the window increases.","The warm air from the defrosting vent for the front windshield cools rapidly as it dissipates throughout the rest of the car.","The defrosting vent operates efficiently even when the heater, which blows warm air toward the feet or faces of the driver and passengers, is on."],"ans":1,"exp":"If the back window — with no vent blowing on it — melted just as fast, something other than the vent explains the melting (B). A is irrelevant, C supports the explanation, and D and E do not cast doubt on the vent being the cause.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-115"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"A conservation group in the United States is trying to change the long-standing image of bats as frightening creatures. The group contends that bats are feared and persecuted solely because they are shy animals that are active only at night.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, would cast the most serious doubt on the accuracy of the group’s contention?","opts":["Bats are steadily losing natural roosting places such as caves and hollow trees and are thus turning to more developed areas for roosting.","Bats are the chief consumers of nocturnal insects and thus can help make their hunting territory more pleasant for humans.","Bats are regarded as frightening creatures not only in the United States but also in Europe, Africa, and South America.","Raccoons and owls are shy and active only at night; yet they are not generally feared and persecuted.","People know more about the behaviour of other greatly feared animal species, such as lions, alligators, and snakes, than they do about the behaviour of bats."],"ans":3,"exp":"If shyness and nocturnal habits alone caused fear, raccoons and owls would be feared too. They are not (D), so those traits cannot be the sole reason. The other options do not test the claimed cause.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-116"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Opponents of laws that require automobile drivers and passengers to wear seat belts argue that in a free society people have the right to take risks as long as they do not harm others as a result of taking the risks. As a result, they conclude that it should be each person’s decision whether or not to wear a seat belt.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the conclusion drawn above?","opts":["Many new cars are built with seat belts that automatically fasten when someone sits in the front seat.","Automobile insurance rates for all automobile owners are higher because of the need to pay for the increased injuries or deaths of people not wearing seat belts.","Passengers in airplanes are required to wear seat belts during takeoffs and landings.","The rate of automobile fatalities in states that do not have mandatory seat belt laws is greater than the rate of fatalities in states that do have such laws.","In automobile accidents, a greater number of passengers who do not wear seat belts are injured than are passengers who do wear seat belts."],"ans":1,"exp":"The argument depends on not wearing a seat belt harming no one else. B shows it does harm others — through higher insurance costs for everyone — so the principle no longer supports the conclusion. D and E only confirm the personal risk, which the argument already concedes.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-117"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"During the Second World War, about 375,000 civilians died in the United States and about 408,000 members of the United States armed forces died overseas. On the basis of those figures, it can be concluded that it was not much more dangerous to be overseas in the armed forces during the Second World War than it was to stay at home as a civilian.\n\nWhich of the following would reveal most clearly the absurdity of the conclusion drawn above?","opts":["Counting deaths among members of the armed forces who served in the United States in addition to deaths among members of the armed forces serving overseas","Expressing the difference between the numbers of deaths among civilians and members of the armed forces as a percentage of the total number of deaths","Separating deaths caused by accidents during service in the armed forces from deaths caused by combat injuries","Comparing death rates per thousand members of each group rather than comparing total numbers of deaths","Comparing deaths caused by accidents in the United States to deaths caused by combat in the armed forces"],"ans":3,"exp":"The civilian population was vastly larger than the forces overseas, so similar totals hide very different risks. Comparing death rates per thousand (D) exposes this. The other options add groups or split causes without addressing group size.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-118"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":4,"exp":"Rule 1: the shape alternates between circle and square. Rule 2: the line outside the shape moves clockwise one corner, then two, then three, and so on. Rule 3: the line inside the shape rotates 45° clockwise each time. That gives E.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_q21.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-119"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":4,"exp":"Rule 1: the flag moves down one place each time, restarting from the top. Rule 2: the flag alternates between pointing left and right. Rule 3: the line in the flag rotates 45° anticlockwise each time. That gives E.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_q22.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-120"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":2,"exp":"Rule 1: the shaded circle moves down one place each time, restarting from the top. Rule 2: the crossed circle moves one place right, restarting from the left. Rule 3: the blank circle moves one place clockwise. That gives C.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_q23.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-121"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":3,"exp":"Rule 1: the square rotates 45° each time. Rule 2: both arrows move one place clockwise around the box. Rule 3: the arrows always point in opposite directions. That gives D.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_q24.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-122"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":1,"exp":"Rule 1: the shape rotates 90° anticlockwise each time. Rule 2: it alternates between shaded and unshaded. Rule 3: it moves from top-left to middle-right to bottom-left, then repeats. That gives B.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_q25.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-123"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":0,"exp":"Rule 1: the shaded block moves one place right each time, restarting from the left. Rule 2: the cross alternates between the top and bottom of the shape. Rule 3: the circle moves one place clockwise every two boxes. That gives A.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_q26.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-124"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":2,"exp":"Rule 1: the outer circle rotates 45° anticlockwise each time. Rule 2: the inner shape rotates 90° anticlockwise. Rule 3: the inner shape alternates between shaded and unshaded. That gives C.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_q27.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-125"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":3,"exp":"Rule 1: the horizontally barred square moves one place clockwise around the box. Rule 2: its bars alternate between shaded and unshaded. Rule 3: the vertically barred square moves up one place each time, restarting at the bottom. That gives D.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_q28.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-126"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":2,"exp":"Rule 1: the rows of triangles move clockwise to the next edge each time, with the row of six always nearest the edge. Rule 2: the triangles alternate between pointing to the centre and pointing away. Rule 3: the number of shaded triangles increases by one each time. That gives C.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_q29.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-127"},{"cat":"Abstract Reasoning","q":"Which figure comes next in the sequence?","opts":["Figure A","Figure B","Figure C","Figure D","Figure E"],"ans":4,"exp":"Rule 1: the small circle moves anticlockwise one place, then two, then three. Rule 2: the middle circle moves one place clockwise around the large circle. Rule 3: the shading cycles large, middle, small. That gives E.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_q30.png","imgLabel":"Sequence and answer options","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-128"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"How much V.A.T must Wisdom Cyber Café pay to FIRS for 2012?","opts":["₦22,000","₦23,000","₦24,000","₦25,000","₦26,000"],"ans":3,"exp":"Profit = 550,000 − 50,000 = ₦500,000. V.A.T = 5% × 500,000 = ₦25,000.","passage":"The Federal Inland Revenue Service (FIRS) charges V.A.T at 5% on the annual profit (income − expense) of every business in Nigeria. A business that misses V.A.T for a year and is caught pays 7% for the missed year(s) and for the present year.\n\n| Business | Annual income 2012 (₦) | Annual expense 2012 (₦) |\n| Kings Business Centre | 500,000 | 45,000 |\n| Shakespeare e-Centre | 780,000 | 70,000 |\n| Wisdom Cyber Café | 550,000 | 50,000 |","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-129"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Shakespeare e-Centre avoided V.A.T for 2012 but was caught and penalised. If income and expenses were the same in 2012 and 2013, how much V.A.T would it pay in 2013 (covering both years)?","opts":["₦95,200","₦95,400","₦95,600","₦96,700","₦99,400"],"ans":4,"exp":"Profit = 780,000 − 70,000 = ₦710,000 per year. The 7% penalty rate applies to the missed year and the present year: 2 × 7% × 710,000 = 2 × 49,700 = ₦99,400.","passage":"The Federal Inland Revenue Service (FIRS) charges V.A.T at 5% on the annual profit (income − expense) of every business in Nigeria. A business that misses V.A.T for a year and is caught pays 7% for the missed year(s) and for the present year.\n\n| Business | Annual income 2012 (₦) | Annual expense 2012 (₦) |\n| Kings Business Centre | 500,000 | 45,000 |\n| Shakespeare e-Centre | 780,000 | 70,000 |\n| Wisdom Cyber Café | 550,000 | 50,000 |","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-130"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Kings Business Centre’s annual expenses fell by 10% in 2013 while its income stayed the same. How much V.A.T would it pay for 2013?","opts":["₦22,975","₦23,500","₦27,750","₦28,450","₦28,750"],"ans":0,"exp":"Expenses = 45,000 × 0.9 = ₦40,500. Profit = 500,000 − 40,500 = ₦459,500. V.A.T = 5% × 459,500 = ₦22,975.","passage":"The Federal Inland Revenue Service (FIRS) charges V.A.T at 5% on the annual profit (income − expense) of every business in Nigeria. A business that misses V.A.T for a year and is caught pays 7% for the missed year(s) and for the present year.\n\n| Business | Annual income 2012 (₦) | Annual expense 2012 (₦) |\n| Kings Business Centre | 500,000 | 45,000 |\n| Shakespeare e-Centre | 780,000 | 70,000 |\n| Wisdom Cyber Café | 550,000 | 50,000 |","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-131"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Kings Business Centre mistakenly paid ₦32,000 V.A.T for 2012. By how much does this differ from the correct amount?","opts":["₦8,620","₦9,000","₦9,250","₦10,150","₦10,250"],"ans":2,"exp":"Profit = 500,000 − 45,000 = ₦455,000. Correct V.A.T = 5% × 455,000 = ₦22,750. Difference = 32,000 − 22,750 = ₦9,250.","passage":"The Federal Inland Revenue Service (FIRS) charges V.A.T at 5% on the annual profit (income − expense) of every business in Nigeria. A business that misses V.A.T for a year and is caught pays 7% for the missed year(s) and for the present year.\n\n| Business | Annual income 2012 (₦) | Annual expense 2012 (₦) |\n| Kings Business Centre | 500,000 | 45,000 |\n| Shakespeare e-Centre | 780,000 | 70,000 |\n| Wisdom Cyber Café | 550,000 | 50,000 |","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-132"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Wisdom Cyber Café’s annual income rises by 10% a year and its expenses by 5% a year for 2 years. What V.A.T will it pay for 2014?","opts":["₦30,493.75","₦33,534.87","₦35,675.66","₦38,778.77","₦39,927.45"],"ans":0,"exp":"Income: 550,000 × 1.1² = ₦665,500. Expenses: 50,000 × 1.05² = ₦55,125. Profit = ₦610,375. V.A.T = 5% × 610,375 = ₦30,518.75 — closest option ₦30,493.75.","passage":"The Federal Inland Revenue Service (FIRS) charges V.A.T at 5% on the annual profit (income − expense) of every business in Nigeria. A business that misses V.A.T for a year and is caught pays 7% for the missed year(s) and for the present year.\n\n| Business | Annual income 2012 (₦) | Annual expense 2012 (₦) |\n| Kings Business Centre | 500,000 | 45,000 |\n| Shakespeare e-Centre | 780,000 | 70,000 |\n| Wisdom Cyber Café | 550,000 | 50,000 |","note":"The source does not name the business; Wisdom Cyber Café matches the source working. The source also subtracted ₦55,125 from ₦665,500 incorrectly (₦609,875 instead of ₦610,375), which gives the listed ₦30,493.75; the closest option is kept.","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-133"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Which product had the lowest value of sales per non-European store, and which had the highest value of sales per European store?","opts":["PU10 (non-European); AE25 (European)","FD24 (non-European); DE45 (European)","FD24 (non-European); AE25 (European)","AE25 (non-European); PU10 (European)","AE25 (non-European); FD24 (European)"],"ans":2,"exp":"Per non-European store: DE45 2,500; PU20 2,222; AE25 2,167; PU10 4,800; FD24 1,286 → lowest FD24. Per European store: DE45 808; PU20 1,579; AE25 2,227; PU10 2,078; FD24 1,131 → highest AE25.","passage":"The tables show, for five products, the number of stores, current month’s sales and price per unit in non-European stores ($) and European stores (€).","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_products.png","imgLabel":"Product sales tables","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-134"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"What is the difference (in $) between the AE25 price per unit in non-European stores and in European stores? Use an exchange rate of €0.80 to the $.","opts":["$30","$120","$130","$200","$230"],"ans":1,"exp":"Non-European price = $130. European price = €200 ÷ 0.80 = $250. Difference = $120.","passage":"The tables show, for five products, the number of stores, current month’s sales and price per unit in non-European stores ($) and European stores (€).","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_products.png","imgLabel":"Product sales tables","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-135"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"This month’s combined target for non-European and European sales of AE25 is €40,000. Using an exchange rate of €0.75 to the $, what is the shortfall against this target?","opts":["575","750","5,100","5,750","7,500"],"ans":3,"exp":"Non-European AE25 sales: $13,000 × 0.75 = €9,750. Combined with €24,500 = €34,250. Shortfall = 40,000 − 34,250 = €5,750.","passage":"The tables show, for five products, the number of stores, current month’s sales and price per unit in non-European stores ($) and European stores (€).","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_products.png","imgLabel":"Product sales tables","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-136"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Combining European and non-European sales, which product sold the most units? Use the non-promotional prices shown.","opts":["DE45","PU20","AE25","PU10","FD24"],"ans":0,"exp":"Units = sales ÷ price. DE45: 200 + 140 = 340; PU20: 100 + 187.5 = 287.5; AE25: 100 + 122.5 = 222.5; PU10: 160 + 170 = 330; FD24: 50 + 163.3 = 213.3. DE45 sold the most.","passage":"The tables show, for five products, the number of stores, current month’s sales and price per unit in non-European stores ($) and European stores (€).","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_products.png","imgLabel":"Product sales tables","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-137"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If a customer uses the promotional offers shown, put the 5 products sold in non-European stores in order of increasing promotional price per unit (cheapest first).","opts":["AE25, PU10, DE45, FD24, PU20","PU10, DE45, PU20, AE25, FD24","PU10, DE45, AE25, PU20, FD24","DE45, PU10, PU20, AE25, FD24","PU10, DE45, PU20, FD24, AE25"],"ans":2,"exp":"PU10 (4 for 3) $112.50; DE45 (3 for 2) $116.67; AE25 (no offer) $130; PU20 (3 for 2) $133.33; FD24 (4 for 3) $135.","passage":"The tables show, for five products, the number of stores, current month’s sales and price per unit in non-European stores ($) and European stores (€).","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_products.png","imgLabel":"Product sales tables","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-138"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"For which three-year period and subsidiary was the average number of employees 1,553?","opts":["2005–2007, Subsidiary 1","2006–2008, Subsidiary 1","2007–2009, Subsidiary 4","2007–2009, Subsidiary 1","None of these"],"ans":2,"exp":"Subsidiary 4, 2007–2009: (1,528 + 1,548 + 1,583) ÷ 3 = 1,553. The others average 1,565, 1,581 and 1,591.","passage":"The table shows the number of employees in a parent company’s five subsidiaries (2005–2009) and the percentage of the parent company’s employees working part-time. The parent company’s workforce is made up only of its subsidiaries’ employees.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_employees.png","imgLabel":"Employees by subsidiary","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-139"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"In 2008 Subsidiary 4 had 2 regions, one with double the employees of the other. If the male : female ratio in the smaller region was 1 : 1.15, how many male employees did that region have?","opts":["240","828","414","394","360"],"ans":0,"exp":"Smaller region = 1,548 ÷ 3 = 516 employees. Males = 516 ÷ 2.15 = 240.","passage":"The table shows the number of employees in a parent company’s five subsidiaries (2005–2009) and the percentage of the parent company’s employees working part-time. The parent company’s workforce is made up only of its subsidiaries’ employees.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_employees.png","imgLabel":"Employees by subsidiary","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-140"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"1 in 15 of the parent company’s part-time employees were managers in 2005, and 1 in 13 in 2007. How did the number of part-time managers in 2005 compare with 2007?","opts":["14 fewer","12 more","12 fewer","13 more","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"2005: total 6,500 × 12% = 780 part-time → 780 ÷ 15 = 52 managers. 2007: total 6,500 × 8% = 520 → 520 ÷ 13 = 40. 2005 had 12 more.","passage":"The table shows the number of employees in a parent company’s five subsidiaries (2005–2009) and the percentage of the parent company’s employees working part-time. The parent company’s workforce is made up only of its subsidiaries’ employees.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_employees.png","imgLabel":"Employees by subsidiary","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-141"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"What percentage of the parent company’s employees worked for Subsidiary 5 in 2006 (to the nearest whole %)?","opts":["12%","10%","18%","15%","9%"],"ans":3,"exp":"2006 total = 1,584 + 1,084 + 1,384 + 1,495 + 980 = 6,527. 980 ÷ 6,527 = 15.0%.","passage":"The table shows the number of employees in a parent company’s five subsidiaries (2005–2009) and the percentage of the parent company’s employees working part-time. The parent company’s workforce is made up only of its subsidiaries’ employees.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_employees.png","imgLabel":"Employees by subsidiary","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-142"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"In 2009, what was the difference between the parent company’s full-time and part-time employees?","opts":["6,270","90","4,733","6,600","5,940"],"ans":4,"exp":"2009 total = 6,600; part-time 5% = 330; full-time 6,270. Difference = 6,270 − 330 = 5,940.","passage":"The table shows the number of employees in a parent company’s five subsidiaries (2005–2009) and the percentage of the parent company’s employees working part-time. The parent company’s workforce is made up only of its subsidiaries’ employees.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_employees.png","imgLabel":"Employees by subsidiary","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-143"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"What was the approximate fraction of Fixed Assets to Loans Made at the end of 2009?","opts":["1/40","1/45","1/20","1/60","1/48"],"ans":0,"exp":"614 ÷ 24,600 ≈ 0.025 = 1/40.","passage":"The table shows assets and liabilities (£ million) at the end of the financial years 2007, 2008 and 2009.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_balance.png","imgLabel":"Assets and liabilities","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-144"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Which asset or assets changed in value by more than 12% from 2007 to 2009?","opts":["Liquid Assets, Loans Made","Loans Made, Fixed Assets","Loans Made","Fixed Assets","Can’t tell from data"],"ans":3,"exp":"Liquid Assets 10,735 → 10,214 (−4.9%); Loans Made 21,974 → 24,600 (+11.95%); Fixed Assets 542 → 614 (+13.3%). Only Fixed Assets exceeds 12%.","passage":"The table shows assets and liabilities (£ million) at the end of the financial years 2007, 2008 and 2009.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_balance.png","imgLabel":"Assets and liabilities","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-145"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"In 2010, Loans Made are projected to fall by an eighth while Derivatives and Fixed Assets each rise by 5%. What is the effect on Total Assets (£ million), compared with 2009?","opts":["3,075.70 increase","3,018.70 decrease","3,000.00 decrease","3,095.70 decrease","Can’t tell from data"],"ans":1,"exp":"Loans: −24,600 ÷ 8 = −3,075. Derivatives: +512 × 5% = +25.6. Fixed Assets: +614 × 5% = +30.7. Net change = −3,018.7.","passage":"The table shows assets and liabilities (£ million) at the end of the financial years 2007, 2008 and 2009.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_balance.png","imgLabel":"Assets and liabilities","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-146"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Which liability or liabilities changed by 10% between 2008 and 2009?","opts":["Reserve Liabilities","Borrowings, Reserve Liabilities","Borrowings","Other Liabilities, Borrowings","Other Liabilities, Share Liabilities"],"ans":1,"exp":"Reserve 124 → 111.6 (−10%); Borrowings 1,544 → 1,389.6 (−10%); Share 1,628 → 1,958 (+20%); Other 35 → 41.8 (+19%).","passage":"The table shows assets and liabilities (£ million) at the end of the financial years 2007, 2008 and 2009.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_balance.png","imgLabel":"Assets and liabilities","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-147"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"What is the ratio of Reserve Liabilities 2008 : Reserve Liabilities 2007?","opts":["132:124","13:12","12:13","31:33","31:32"],"ans":3,"exp":"124 : 132 — divide both by 4 → 31 : 33.","passage":"The table shows assets and liabilities (£ million) at the end of the financial years 2007, 2008 and 2009.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf3_balance.png","imgLabel":"Assets and liabilities","src":"Workforce Test 3.0","id":"workforce-148"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"The best approach to PR is to be flexible, regularly changing the company's goals to keep the public interested.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"The passage says “The perfect PR message will be consistent, with each new approach reinforcing the key objectives of the company.” Changing the goals contradicts this.","passage":"An effective PR campaign requires precise, clear communication between the client and PR officer. The client should disclose detailed information to the PR officer, including the company's history, goals, and current business plan. It is especially important to disclose any potentially problematic issues. The company should be prepared to dedicate the necessary time and resources of its senior management, as well as sufficient finances, to the campaign.\n\nThe perfect PR message will be consistent, with each new approach reinforcing the key objectives of the company. If new developments do arise, the PR officer should be fully briefed as soon as possible. It is essential to keep to a clear schedule, leaving adequate time available for approval of copy. Seizing opportunities when they arise is key to the success of the campaign.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-149"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Not disclosing a full company history to the PR officer will result in a failed campaign.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"The passage says the client should disclose the company history, but never says that failing to do so will by itself make the campaign fail.","passage":"An effective PR campaign requires precise, clear communication between the client and PR officer. The client should disclose detailed information to the PR officer, including the company's history, goals, and current business plan. It is especially important to disclose any potentially problematic issues. The company should be prepared to dedicate the necessary time and resources of its senior management, as well as sufficient finances, to the campaign.\n\nThe perfect PR message will be consistent, with each new approach reinforcing the key objectives of the company. If new developments do arise, the PR officer should be fully briefed as soon as possible. It is essential to keep to a clear schedule, leaving adequate time available for approval of copy. Seizing opportunities when they arise is key to the success of the campaign.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-150"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"It is recommended to wait before taking advantage of any new opportunities, to make certain they would be of benefit to the campaign.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"The passage says “Seizing opportunities when they arise is key to the success of the campaign” — the opposite of waiting.","passage":"An effective PR campaign requires precise, clear communication between the client and PR officer. The client should disclose detailed information to the PR officer, including the company's history, goals, and current business plan. It is especially important to disclose any potentially problematic issues. The company should be prepared to dedicate the necessary time and resources of its senior management, as well as sufficient finances, to the campaign.\n\nThe perfect PR message will be consistent, with each new approach reinforcing the key objectives of the company. If new developments do arise, the PR officer should be fully briefed as soon as possible. It is essential to keep to a clear schedule, leaving adequate time available for approval of copy. Seizing opportunities when they arise is key to the success of the campaign.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-151"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Knowledge of a company's reputation is not useful for salespeople.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"The passage says “It is also useful to know … its present reputation.”","passage":"A good salesperson should always learn something about the company, and even the individuals, behind the product he or she is selling. Confidence in a product depends in part on confidence in the integrity, competence, and commitment of those who manufacture and distribute that product. Salespeople should therefore familiarise themselves with the principal personalities behind a company, gaining an understanding of its personnel structure and the functions, duties, and experience of key individuals within the business. It is also useful to know something of the history and development of the company, as well as being aware of its present reputation, and to be familiar with the company's particular practices and policies. As well as providing a more thorough knowledge of the product, this information can help to form the basis of an effective sales pitch.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-152"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"The personal traits and abilities of a company's personnel can influence the confidence people have in their product.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"“Confidence in a product depends in part on confidence in the integrity, competence, and commitment of those who manufacture and distribute that product.” Integrity, competence and commitment are personal traits and abilities.","passage":"A good salesperson should always learn something about the company, and even the individuals, behind the product he or she is selling. Confidence in a product depends in part on confidence in the integrity, competence, and commitment of those who manufacture and distribute that product. Salespeople should therefore familiarise themselves with the principal personalities behind a company, gaining an understanding of its personnel structure and the functions, duties, and experience of key individuals within the business. It is also useful to know something of the history and development of the company, as well as being aware of its present reputation, and to be familiar with the company's particular practices and policies. As well as providing a more thorough knowledge of the product, this information can help to form the basis of an effective sales pitch.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-153"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"It is helpful to have knowledge of the background, policies and reputation of a company when developing an effective sales pitch.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"After describing knowledge of the company’s history, reputation, practices and policies, the passage says “this information can help to form the basis of an effective sales pitch.”","passage":"A good salesperson should always learn something about the company, and even the individuals, behind the product he or she is selling. Confidence in a product depends in part on confidence in the integrity, competence, and commitment of those who manufacture and distribute that product. Salespeople should therefore familiarise themselves with the principal personalities behind a company, gaining an understanding of its personnel structure and the functions, duties, and experience of key individuals within the business. It is also useful to know something of the history and development of the company, as well as being aware of its present reputation, and to be familiar with the company's particular practices and policies. As well as providing a more thorough knowledge of the product, this information can help to form the basis of an effective sales pitch.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-154"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"CCTV surveillance is an invasion of privacy.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"The passage only says the question of confidentiality has been raised, and that in public places it is harder to argue that CCTV is an invasion of privacy. It never settles the issue.","passage":"Levels of passive surveillance have increased recently. For example, many companies monitor their employees’ use of the Internet or of any campaign group activities that may have operational implications. State surveillance now processes huge amounts of information that is collected from a variety of sources to produce profiles of individuals and groups. There are now reportedly more closed-circuit television (CCTV) cameras per head of the UK population than any other country in the world. Such a rapid expansion of CCTV surveillance in particular has raised questions about whether this is economically justifiable, whether one’s right to confidentiality is compromised and whether the public are in support of it. In public places it is harder to argue that video surveillance is an invasion of privacy. The sheer presence of CCTV cameras should deter some criminals when they know that police officers are surveying their actions. However, some criminologists claim that criminal activity is simply diverted elsewhere. CCTV footage may offer unbiased court evidence and so contribute towards keeping many criminals off the streets, but footage needs to be stored securely – even if most of what is filmed does not get to be seen.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-155"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Public security, CCTV costs and an individual’s right to privacy are three factors raised within the passage.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"All three are raised: deterring criminals (security), whether CCTV is “economically justifiable” (cost) and whether “one’s right to confidentiality is compromised” (privacy).","passage":"Levels of passive surveillance have increased recently. For example, many companies monitor their employees’ use of the Internet or of any campaign group activities that may have operational implications. State surveillance now processes huge amounts of information that is collected from a variety of sources to produce profiles of individuals and groups. There are now reportedly more closed-circuit television (CCTV) cameras per head of the UK population than any other country in the world. Such a rapid expansion of CCTV surveillance in particular has raised questions about whether this is economically justifiable, whether one’s right to confidentiality is compromised and whether the public are in support of it. In public places it is harder to argue that video surveillance is an invasion of privacy. The sheer presence of CCTV cameras should deter some criminals when they know that police officers are surveying their actions. However, some criminologists claim that criminal activity is simply diverted elsewhere. CCTV footage may offer unbiased court evidence and so contribute towards keeping many criminals off the streets, but footage needs to be stored securely – even if most of what is filmed does not get to be seen.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-156"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"It is difficult to justify CCTV surveillance if most of the footage remains unseen.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"The passage mentions that most footage is never seen, but only in connection with secure storage. It does not say this makes CCTV hard to justify.","passage":"Levels of passive surveillance have increased recently. For example, many companies monitor their employees’ use of the Internet or of any campaign group activities that may have operational implications. State surveillance now processes huge amounts of information that is collected from a variety of sources to produce profiles of individuals and groups. There are now reportedly more closed-circuit television (CCTV) cameras per head of the UK population than any other country in the world. Such a rapid expansion of CCTV surveillance in particular has raised questions about whether this is economically justifiable, whether one’s right to confidentiality is compromised and whether the public are in support of it. In public places it is harder to argue that video surveillance is an invasion of privacy. The sheer presence of CCTV cameras should deter some criminals when they know that police officers are surveying their actions. However, some criminologists claim that criminal activity is simply diverted elsewhere. CCTV footage may offer unbiased court evidence and so contribute towards keeping many criminals off the streets, but footage needs to be stored securely – even if most of what is filmed does not get to be seen.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-157"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"The public’s main concerns about increases in surveillance have been around CCTV.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"Source explanation: this can be inferred from the fifth sentence’s “in particular” — the expansion of CCTV “in particular” has raised the questions.","passage":"Levels of passive surveillance have increased recently. For example, many companies monitor their employees’ use of the Internet or of any campaign group activities that may have operational implications. State surveillance now processes huge amounts of information that is collected from a variety of sources to produce profiles of individuals and groups. There are now reportedly more closed-circuit television (CCTV) cameras per head of the UK population than any other country in the world. Such a rapid expansion of CCTV surveillance in particular has raised questions about whether this is economically justifiable, whether one’s right to confidentiality is compromised and whether the public are in support of it. In public places it is harder to argue that video surveillance is an invasion of privacy. The sheer presence of CCTV cameras should deter some criminals when they know that police officers are surveying their actions. However, some criminologists claim that criminal activity is simply diverted elsewhere. CCTV footage may offer unbiased court evidence and so contribute towards keeping many criminals off the streets, but footage needs to be stored securely – even if most of what is filmed does not get to be seen.","note":"The source answer key (True) is used here. Worked independently, the answer comes out as Cannot say: The passage says the expansion of CCTV “in particular” has raised questions, but it does not say who raised them, and it lists “whether the public are in support of it” as an open question. Nothing tells us what the public’s main concerns are.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-158"},{"cat":"Verbal Ability","q":"Corporal punishment is still used in some schools as a “deterrent” to undisciplined students.","opts":["Remedy","Measure","Discouragement","Prevention","Hindrance"],"ans":2,"exp":"A deterrent is something that discourages an action through fear of the consequences, so “discouragement” is the closest replacement.","note":"Choose the option closest in meaning to the expression in quotation marks.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-159"},{"cat":"Verbal Ability","q":"On crossing the bridge, the commander ordered it to be burnt, telling his soldiers that they had “crossed the Rubicon”.","opts":["Reached a point of no return","Attained a commendable feat","Excelled in the war","Gotten to an end point","Passed their boundary"],"ans":0,"exp":"To “cross the Rubicon” (after Julius Caesar) is to take an irreversible step. Burning the bridge makes retreat impossible, so they have reached a point of no return.","note":"Choose the option closest in meaning to the expression in quotation marks.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-160"},{"cat":"Verbal Ability","q":"I am sure you didn’t know that the lady is “light-fingered”. This means that the lady is","opts":["Careless in handling things","Fair-skinned","Prone to stealing","Skilful with her hands","Swift in boxing"],"ans":2,"exp":"“Light-fingered” describes someone who habitually steals small things.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-161"},{"cat":"Verbal Ability","q":"After the investigation, many of the workers were given their “marching orders”. This means that the workers","opts":["Were dismissed","Were ordered to march","Received fresh instructions about their job","Were given orders similar to the first one","Were given passing-out letters"],"ans":0,"exp":"To be given your marching orders is to be dismissed from a job.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-162"},{"cat":"Verbal Ability","q":"To drive on through the red light is “an infringement” of the law.","opts":["A trespass","An offence","A breach","An invasion","A brute"],"ans":2,"exp":"An infringement is a violation of a law or rule: “a breach of the law”. “An offence of the law” and “a trespass of the law” are not idiomatic.","note":"The source explanation names “A breach” but labels it option A; in its own option list “A breach” is option C.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-163"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"What was the average annual income across the four years shown (to the nearest million)?","opts":["£408 million","£407 million","£402 million","£403 million","£404 million"],"ans":2,"exp":"Income = net interest + other income + fair value gains. 2009: 407.4; 2008: 401.2; 2007: 401.4; 2006: 396.6. Average = 1,606.6 ÷ 4 = 401.65 ≈ £402 million.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf4_income.png","imgLabel":"Gills & Tines Ltd — UK operations (£million)","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-164"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Gills & Tines Ltd’s target has been to increase Profit Before Tax by more than 2% each year. In which year, or years, has this been achieved?","opts":["2008","2007, 2008","2007","2007, 2008, 2009","None of the years shown"],"ans":1,"exp":"2009: (114.6 − 112.4) ÷ 112.4 = 1.96% (missed). 2008: (112.4 − 109.4) ÷ 109.4 = 2.74%. 2007: (109.4 − 107.2) ÷ 107.2 = 2.05%. The target was met in 2007 and 2008.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf4_income.png","imgLabel":"Gills & Tines Ltd — UK operations (£million)","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-165"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Admin costs are projected to increase by a quarter in 2010 and Net Interest to increase by 2.5%, whilst all other costs and incomes are projected to remain constant. What is the projected Profit Before Tax for 2010?","opts":["£53.28 million","£69.45 million","£113.2 million","£144.6 million","£118.9 million"],"ans":0,"exp":"Extra admin cost = 25% × 277.8 = 69.45. Extra net interest = 2.5% × 325.2 = 8.13. PBT 2010 = 114.6 − 69.45 + 8.13 = £53.28 million.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf4_income.png","imgLabel":"Gills & Tines Ltd — UK operations (£million)","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-166"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"In which year did the combined Admin Costs and Loan Impairment Costs decrease in value?","opts":["2006","2007","2008","2009","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Combined costs: 2006 289.4, 2007 292.0, 2008 288.8, 2009 292.8. The only fall is from 292.0 (2007) to 288.8 (2008).","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf4_income.png","imgLabel":"Gills & Tines Ltd — UK operations (£million)","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-167"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If corporation tax of 21% was applied each year to the Profit Before Tax, what was the average net profit across 2006–2009?","opts":["£110.9 million","£114.6 million","£115.6 million","£86.4 million","£87.6 million"],"ans":4,"exp":"Average PBT = (114.6 + 112.4 + 109.4 + 107.2) ÷ 4 = 110.9. Net profit = 79% × 110.9 = £87.6 million.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf4_income.png","imgLabel":"Gills & Tines Ltd — UK operations (£million)","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-168"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Which department has the highest expense budget per member of staff?","opts":["HR","Marketing","Sales","IT","Finance"],"ans":4,"exp":"Budget ÷ staff: HR 6,500 ÷ 3 = £2,167; Marketing 16,000 ÷ 6 = £2,667; Sales 22,500 ÷ 12 = £1,875; IT 4,500 ÷ 5 = £900; Finance 20,000 ÷ 7 = £2,857; R&D 6,000 ÷ 4 = £1,500. Finance is highest.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf4_expenses.png","imgLabel":"Expenses by department (£)","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-169"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If the annual expense budget was evenly allocated for each quarter, which department is under budget by the highest amount in Quarter 4?","opts":["HR","Marketing","Sales","Finance","R&D"],"ans":3,"exp":"Quarterly budget minus Q4 spend: HR 1,625 − 1,346 = £279; Marketing 4,000 − 4,309 (over); Sales 5,625 − 5,245 = £380; Finance 5,000 − 4,463 = £537; R&D 1,500 − 1,821 (over). Finance is furthest under budget.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf4_expenses.png","imgLabel":"Expenses by department (£)","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-170"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"60% of the Sales Department’s budgets for Quarters 1 and 4 was for attending a Sales Conference. The remainder of the budget was split equally between accommodation and travel costs. What were the Sales Department’s travel costs for Quarters 1 and 4 combined?","opts":["£2,414","£2,500","£3,500","£4,828","Can’t tell from the data"],"ans":4,"exp":"The table gives only the annual budget and the actual quarterly expenses. Nothing says how the budget was split between quarters, so the Q1 and Q4 budgets cannot be found.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf4_expenses.png","imgLabel":"Expenses by department (£)","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-171"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"The Finance Department has receipts for £14,476 of its annual expenses. What percentage of the Finance Department’s annual expenses do not have receipts?","opts":["5%","10%","15%","20%","25%"],"ans":3,"exp":"Finance expenses = 4,257 + 4,830 + 4,545 + 4,463 = £18,095. 14,476 ÷ 18,095 = 80% have receipts, so 20% do not.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf4_expenses.png","imgLabel":"Expenses by department (£)","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-172"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If the percentage changes in expenses that each department exhibited between Quarters 3 and 4 continued into the first quarter of the next year, what would be that quarter’s total expenses (to the nearest £100)?","opts":["£17,100","£19,100","£19,600","£20,600","None of these"],"ans":1,"exp":"Next quarter = Q4 × (Q4 ÷ Q3) for each department: HR 1,346 × 1.0314 = 1,388; Marketing 4,309 × 1.1799 = 5,084; Sales 5,245 × 1.0302 = 5,404; IT 956 × 1.0192 = 974; Finance 4,463 × 0.9820 = 4,383; R&D 1,821 × 1.0376 = 1,889. Total ≈ £19,122, i.e. £19,100.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf4_expenses.png","imgLabel":"Expenses by department (£)","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-173"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Judy is now twice as old as Adam, but 6 years ago she was 5 times as old as he was. How old is Judy now?","opts":["10","16","20","24","32"],"ans":1,"exp":"Let Adam = A, so Judy = 2A. Six years ago: 2A − 6 = 5(A − 6), so 2A − 6 = 5A − 30 and A = 8. Judy = 16.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-174"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Sharon read 24 pages of her book in 15 minutes. At this rate, how many pages can she read in 40 minutes?","opts":["64","66","72","74","76"],"ans":0,"exp":"24 ÷ 15 = 1.6 pages per minute. 1.6 × 40 = 64 pages.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-175"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If 4 boys can shovel a driveway in 2 hours, how many minutes will 5 boys take to do the job?","opts":["86","96","106","116","126"],"ans":1,"exp":"The job takes 4 × 2 = 8 boy-hours. 5 boys need 8 ÷ 5 = 1.6 hours = 96 minutes.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-176"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Of the 410 students at Kennedy High School, 240 study Spanish and 180 study French. If 25 students study neither language, how many students study both?","opts":["25","30","35","40","45"],"ans":2,"exp":"Students taking at least one language = 410 − 25 = 385. Both = 240 + 180 − 385 = 35.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-177"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Brian gave 20% of his baseball cards to Scott and 15% to Adam. If he still had 520 cards, how many did he have originally?","opts":["720","750","780","800","840"],"ans":3,"exp":"He gave away 35%, so 520 cards are 65% of the original. 520 ÷ 0.65 = 800.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-178"},{"cat":"General Knowledge","q":"A customer presents a withdrawal slip and the banker writes “D.A.R.” on it. What does D.A.R. stand for in banking terms?","opts":["Depositor’s Attention Required","Direct Attention Required","Delayed Access Remittances","Deduct Applied Rate","Drawer’s Attention Required"],"ans":0,"exp":"This is the answer given in the source document’s answer key.","note":"The source answer key (Depositor’s Attention Required) is used here. Worked independently, the answer comes out as E) Drawer’s Attention Required: D.A.R. (“Drawer’s Attention Required”) is a return remark meaning the person who wrote the cheque or slip must correct or confirm something (signature, amount or alteration) before it can be paid.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-179"},{"cat":"General Knowledge","q":"On what date did Mallam Sanusi Lamido Sanusi resume office as Governor of the Central Bank of Nigeria?","opts":["June 4, 2009","June 14, 2009","July 4, 2009","July 14, 2009","August 14, 2009"],"ans":0,"exp":"Sanusi was confirmed by the Senate and took office at the start of June 2009, succeeding Chukwuma Soludo, and served until February 2014.","note":"The source text says “July 4, 2009” but marks option A (June 4). Sanusi took office in early June 2009, so option A is kept.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-180"},{"cat":"General Knowledge","q":"The bank recapitalisation exercise reduced the number of operating commercial banks from what number to 24?","opts":["87","88","89","98","99"],"ans":2,"exp":"The 2004–2005 consolidation, which required ₦25 billion minimum capital, cut Nigeria’s 89 banks down to 25, and later to 24.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-181"},{"cat":"General Knowledge","q":"Nigeria’s foreign exchange reserves fell to what range in April–June 2013 before the decline was addressed?","opts":["$56.40 – $52.60 billion","$46.40 – $44.60 billion","$44.60 – $42.40 billion","$42.40 – $40.60 billion","$36.93 – $36.40 billion"],"ans":4,"exp":"This is the answer given in the source document’s answer key.","note":"The source answer key is used here. This item could not be confirmed independently: reported reserve figures for mid-2013 were in the mid-$40 billions, so treat the keyed range with caution.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-182"},{"cat":"General Knowledge","q":"How many litres make a barrel of crude oil?","opts":["159 litres","155 litres","150 litres","148 litres","145 litres"],"ans":0,"exp":"One oil barrel is 42 US gallons, about 158.99 litres (≈159 litres).","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-183"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Olu runs faster than Kunle, Kunle runs faster than Tayo, and Simbi runs faster than Tayo. Which of the options would be right?","opts":["Olu runs faster than Simbi","Tayo runs faster than Olu","Kunle and Simbi run at the same speed","Simbi is the fastest runner","Kunle has two running speeds"],"ans":0,"exp":"Source explanation: O > K > T and S > T. C cannot be confirmed, so the source picks A, Olu runs faster than Simbi.","note":"The source answer key is used here. Worked independently, no option must be true: we only know Olu > Kunle > Tayo and Simbi > Tayo, so Simbi could be faster or slower than Olu.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-184"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Mary: Mr Shola’s children are not okay because they have sickle cell anaemia.\nMartha: No, Mr Shola’s children are okay.\n\nIf anybody that has sickle cell anaemia is considered as not being okay, which of the options would be correct?","opts":["Mary is correct because she sounds clearer","Martha is correct because she is succinct","Neither of them is correct","We cannot say for sure without further information","Both of them are correct based on their view"],"ans":3,"exp":"The rule only says that people with sickle cell anaemia are not okay. Whether the children actually have it is exactly what is disputed, and we are given no evidence either way, so we cannot tell who is right.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-185"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"If there is an oil-supply disruption resulting in higher international oil prices, domestic oil prices in open-market countries such as the United States will rise as well, whether such countries import all or none of their oil.\n\nIf this statement is true, which of the following policies in an open-market nation is most likely to reduce the long-term economic impact on that nation of sharp and unexpected increases in international oil prices?","opts":["Maintaining the quantity of oil imported at constant yearly levels","Increasing the number of oil tankers in its fleet","Suspending diplomatic relations with major oil-producing nations","Decreasing oil consumption through conservation","Decreasing domestic production of oil"],"ans":3,"exp":"Domestic prices rise with world prices whatever the source of the oil, so the only protection is to use less oil. Conservation (D) does that. A and E could increase exposure, and B and C have no link to price impact.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-186"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"People buy prestige when they buy a premium product. They want to be associated with something special. Mass-marketing techniques and price-reduction strategies should not be used because ____.","opts":["Affluent purchasers currently represent a shrinking portion of the population of all purchasers","Continued sales depend directly on the maintenance of an aura of exclusivity","Purchasers of premium products are concerned with the quality as well as with the price of the products","Expansion of the market niche to include a broader spectrum of consumers will increase profits","Manufacturing a premium brand is not necessarily more costly than manufacturing a standard brand of the same product"],"ans":1,"exp":"Mass marketing and discounting make a product less exclusive. If sales depend on exclusivity (B), these tactics would undermine sales. A and D argue for mass marketing, C does not explain the objection, and E is irrelevant.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-187"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"When limitations were in effect on nuclear-arms testing, people tended to save more of their money, but when nuclear-arms testing increased, people tended to spend more of their money. The perceived threat of nuclear catastrophe, therefore, decreases the willingness of people to postpone consumption for the sake of saving money.\n\nThe argument above assumes that","opts":["The perceived threat of nuclear catastrophe has increased over the years","Most people supported the development of nuclear arms","People's perception of the threat of nuclear catastrophe depends on the amount of nuclear-arms testing being done","The people who saved the most money when nuclear-arms testing was limited were the ones who supported such limitations","There are more consumer goods available when nuclear-arms testing increases"],"ans":2,"exp":"The evidence is about the amount of testing, but the conclusion is about the perceived threat. The argument only works if the perceived threat rises and falls with testing (C).","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-188"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Increases in the level of high-density lipoprotein (HDL) in the human bloodstream lower bloodstream-cholesterol levels by increasing the body's capacity to rid itself of excess cholesterol. Levels of HDL in the bloodstream of some individuals are significantly increased by a program of regular exercise and weight reduction.\n\nWhich of the following can be correctly inferred from the statements above?","opts":["Individuals who are underweight do not run any risk of developing high levels of cholesterol in the bloodstream","Individuals who do not exercise regularly have a high risk of developing high levels of cholesterol in the bloodstream late in life","Exercise and weight reduction are the most effective methods of lowering bloodstream cholesterol levels in humans","A program of regular exercise and weight reduction lowers cholesterol levels in the bloodstream of some individuals","Only regular exercise is necessary to decrease cholesterol levels in the bloodstream of individuals of average weight"],"ans":3,"exp":"Exercise and weight loss raise HDL in some people, and higher HDL lowers cholesterol, so the programme lowers cholesterol in some people (D). The other options go beyond the statements (“no risk”, “most effective”, “only exercise”).","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-189"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"A proposed ordinance requires the installation in new homes of sprinklers automatically triggered by the presence of a fire. However, a home builder argued that because more than ninety percent of residential fires are extinguished by a household member, residential sprinklers would only marginally decrease property damage caused by residential fires.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, would most seriously weaken the home builder's argument?","opts":["Most individuals have no formal training in how to extinguish fires","Since new homes are only a tiny percentage of available housing in the city, the new ordinance would be extremely narrow in scope","The installation of smoke detectors in new residences costs significantly less than the installation of sprinklers","In the city where the ordinance was proposed, the average time required by the fire department to respond to a fire was less than the national average","The largest proportion of property damage that results from residential fires is caused by fires that start when no household member is present"],"ans":4,"exp":"The builder counts the number of fires, but the claim is about damage. If most damage comes from the minority of fires where nobody is home (E), sprinklers would prevent a large share of the damage.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-190"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"CROSS8 + TRIANGLE10 = CIRCLE2\nTRIANGLE20 + CROSS14 = SQUARE6\nSQUARE4 + CIRCLE2 = DIAMOND48\n\nCIRCLE6 + SQUARE4 = DIAMOND?\n\nWhat is the value of DIAMOND?","opts":["2","4","6","8","10"],"ans":2,"exp":"Source explanation: −8 + 10 = 2, 20 − 14 = 6, 4 + 2 = 6, so DIAMOND = 6.","note":"The source answer key is used here. Worked independently, the rule is inconsistent: the third example (SQUARE4 + CIRCLE2 = DIAMOND48) does not fit the “add or subtract” pattern the source uses, so the puzzle has no single defensible answer.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-191"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Sue: Commercial flights currently contribute more carbon dioxide to the atmosphere in one year than does the whole of Africa. If we want to reduce global warming we need to restrict the number of flights we take.\nDave: Did you know that by taking one inter-continental flight you cause more pollution than you would in twelve months of car travel?\n\nDave’s response to Sue’s comment serves to","opts":["Reinforce Sue’s contention that flights are a major contributor to increased carbon dioxide levels","Add more weight to her contention that we should reduce the number of flights we take","Mitigate the force of her argument by suggesting that there is an alternative approach","Suggest an alternative that will reduce the effect of pollution","Question whether she really understands the severity of global warming"],"ans":1,"exp":"Dave does not challenge Sue, which rules out C and E. He talks about pollution in general, not carbon dioxide, so A is too specific. His point supports her conclusion that we should take fewer flights (B).","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-192"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"It is not unusual to see the ball fall into a black slot on a roulette wheel four times in a row. But for it to fall five or six times in a row into the same colour is very unusual. Therefore you can win money by waiting for a run of five of the same colour and then betting against that colour.\n\nIf the roulette wheel in question is a fair wheel, which of the following would best reveal a fallacy in the logic?","opts":["If there were a reliable way to win at roulette it would be well-known by now","It is hard for a player to keep track of what went before for the time required","The probability of getting a particular colour decreases with the number of times the colour has appeared","The probability of getting a particular colour is always the same no matter what has gone before","A person who makes money this way once or twice will carry on to lose that money after a few more times"],"ans":3,"exp":"This is the gambler’s fallacy. On a fair wheel each spin is independent, so the chance of a colour is the same whatever came before (D). A long run makes the next spin no less likely to repeat the colour.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-193"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"The committee on sexual discrimination in the workplace has highlighted Supremo Company as a chief offender. Of the twenty senior executives in the firm, only one is a woman. And of the forty junior executives, only five are female.\n\nSupremo could best defend itself against the charges by showing that","opts":["Male and female executives at the same level have the same qualifications","They pay the same salary to senior men and senior women","Ten times more men than women apply for jobs with the company","The work pressures and long hours make jobs with the company unattractive to married women","All job applicants who were rejected had fewer qualifications than those accepted"],"ans":4,"exp":"The charge concerns who gets hired. If every rejected applicant was less qualified than those accepted (E), selection was on merit and not sex. C explains the applicant pool but not whether qualified women were turned down.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-194"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Josh has twenty years of typing experience behind him; therefore, if you are looking for an efficient typist to enter your data into the new system, you need look no further.\n\nThe speaker assumes that","opts":["Twenty years of practice ensures typing efficiency","The type of typing required for the new system is identical to what Josh has been doing","Josh’s job profile is the best that the new employer is going to get","Josh is an outstandingly fast and accurate typist","Josh will fit well into the new office"],"ans":0,"exp":"The only premise is experience and the conclusion is efficiency, so the unstated link is that long practice guarantees efficiency (A). “Identical”, “best” and “outstandingly” go further than the argument needs.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-195"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"It has been suggested that long-term prisoners, on release from jail, be given a reasonable state pension to reduce the likelihood of their resorting to crime. Most people instinctively reject the suggestion as they feel it would be like rewarding criminal activity.\n\nThe supporters of the prisoners’ pension scheme have criticised those who reject this possibility, by claiming that for the critics……","opts":["emotion is more important than justice","punishment for criminals is more important than crime prevention","crime prevention is not an important issue","money has too high a value","the law should not be concerned with what happens after jail"],"ans":1,"exp":"The scheme aims at crime prevention, and the critics reject it because it seems to reward criminals. So, for them, not rewarding (that is, punishing) criminals outweighs preventing crime (B). C goes too far: they may still value prevention.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-196"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Recent studies have highlighted the harmful effects of additives in food (colours, preservatives, flavour enhancers etc.). There are no synthetic substances in the foods we produce at Munchon Foods – we use only natural ingredients. Hence you can be sure you are safeguarding your family’s health when you buy our products.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, would most weaken the contention of Munchon Foods?","opts":["Some synthetic substances are not harmful","Some natural substances found in foods can be harmful","Food without additives is unlikely to taste good","Munchon Foods produces only breakfast cereals","Without preservatives some foods could cause harm"],"ans":1,"exp":"Munchon assumes that “natural” means safe. If natural substances can also be harmful (B), avoiding synthetics does not guarantee healthy food. E also weakens the claim, but less directly than B.","src":"Workforce Test 4.0","id":"workforce-197"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Efforts toward the Planar rocket have not yet begun.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"The passage lists the Planar rocket among the ESA’s “existing activities”, so work on it has already begun.","passage":"The European Space Agency (ESA) is the body which decides the scope and direction of European space initiatives. It is funded by its participating states, currently 18 in number. Each state contributes funding according to its own economic strength. The ESA controls the funding of existing activities such as the Planar rocket and space station projects but is also responsible for the initiation of new projects like the next generation of weather satellites. Some of the ESA projects are compulsory but others are not, in which case member states are free to choose whether they become involved. There is a direct correlation between the amount of money that a member state agrees to invest in a programme and the amount of benefit it can expect in return through contracts awarded to its domestic space industry. ESA directors are keen to maintain a high level of funding from its member states even in times of global economic downturn since they believe that a longer-term view is needed to preserve the reputation of European space technology.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-198"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Financial support provided to the ESA is proportional to each member state’s gross domestic product.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"Each state contributes “according to its own economic strength”. That is not necessarily GDP, and the passage does not say contributions are strictly proportional.","passage":"The European Space Agency (ESA) is the body which decides the scope and direction of European space initiatives. It is funded by its participating states, currently 18 in number. Each state contributes funding according to its own economic strength. The ESA controls the funding of existing activities such as the Planar rocket and space station projects but is also responsible for the initiation of new projects like the next generation of weather satellites. Some of the ESA projects are compulsory but others are not, in which case member states are free to choose whether they become involved. There is a direct correlation between the amount of money that a member state agrees to invest in a programme and the amount of benefit it can expect in return through contracts awarded to its domestic space industry. ESA directors are keen to maintain a high level of funding from its member states even in times of global economic downturn since they believe that a longer-term view is needed to preserve the reputation of European space technology.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-199"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"ESA member states are obliged to contribute to some of the projects managed by the ESA.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"“Some of the ESA projects are compulsory”, so members must take part in (and fund) those projects.","passage":"The European Space Agency (ESA) is the body which decides the scope and direction of European space initiatives. It is funded by its participating states, currently 18 in number. Each state contributes funding according to its own economic strength. The ESA controls the funding of existing activities such as the Planar rocket and space station projects but is also responsible for the initiation of new projects like the next generation of weather satellites. Some of the ESA projects are compulsory but others are not, in which case member states are free to choose whether they become involved. There is a direct correlation between the amount of money that a member state agrees to invest in a programme and the amount of benefit it can expect in return through contracts awarded to its domestic space industry. ESA directors are keen to maintain a high level of funding from its member states even in times of global economic downturn since they believe that a longer-term view is needed to preserve the reputation of European space technology.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-200"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Government investment in green infrastructure projects will promote economic growth.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"This is only what “some commentators argue”. The passage does not state it as a fact, so we cannot say whether such investment will actually promote growth.","passage":"Some commentators argue that in times of global economic uncertainty governments should be boosting flagging economies by investing in major infrastructure projects. Governments should grasp the opportunities presented to tackle global climate change, at the same time as stimulating the economy, by using this public sector investment to move towards a new low-carbon economy. Investment is needed in low-carbon (popularly referred to as ‘green’) projects like renewable energy generation, adapting buildings to be more energy efficient, improving transport networks and even building pipelines to carry captured CO2 to undersea storage facilities. No country is untouched by the effects of climate change and everyone acknowledges that the political will has to be found if we are to see any real changes.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-201"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Many people anticipate that a flagging economy will benefit green projects.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"The commentators say governments should use a downturn to invest in green projects, but nobody is said to expect the weak economy itself to benefit them, and the passage gives no idea how many people hold any view.","passage":"Some commentators argue that in times of global economic uncertainty governments should be boosting flagging economies by investing in major infrastructure projects. Governments should grasp the opportunities presented to tackle global climate change, at the same time as stimulating the economy, by using this public sector investment to move towards a new low-carbon economy. Investment is needed in low-carbon (popularly referred to as ‘green’) projects like renewable energy generation, adapting buildings to be more energy efficient, improving transport networks and even building pipelines to carry captured CO2 to undersea storage facilities. No country is untouched by the effects of climate change and everyone acknowledges that the political will has to be found if we are to see any real changes.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-202"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Some countries are not yet affected by climate change.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"The passage states: “No country is untouched by the effects of climate change.”","passage":"Some commentators argue that in times of global economic uncertainty governments should be boosting flagging economies by investing in major infrastructure projects. Governments should grasp the opportunities presented to tackle global climate change, at the same time as stimulating the economy, by using this public sector investment to move towards a new low-carbon economy. Investment is needed in low-carbon (popularly referred to as ‘green’) projects like renewable energy generation, adapting buildings to be more energy efficient, improving transport networks and even building pipelines to carry captured CO2 to undersea storage facilities. No country is untouched by the effects of climate change and everyone acknowledges that the political will has to be found if we are to see any real changes.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-203"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Offshoring is synonymous with outsourcing.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":1,"exp":"Offshoring is defined as outsourcing “when functions are moved abroad” — a particular kind of outsourcing, not another word for it.","passage":"Outsourcing – purchasing services from an external supplier rather than performing the work internally – is a popular but politically sensitive means of cutting costs. There has been an increasing use of third parties for HR functions, such as managing payroll and other employee data, and for traditional Finance functions, such as invoice services. The manufacture of goods has even become part of this trend, though the design function is typically kept in-house. Third party call centre operatives can offer customer service expertise that may be more expensive to provide in-house. “Offshoring”, when functions are moved abroad, often to India or China, where the average wage is considerably lower, raises job protection issues. The potential profits from outsourcing operations encourage underdeveloped countries to invest in the necessary educational infrastructure and skills training that are required to support such business. Still, higher corporate profits may be seen to be at the expense of low-wage economies, and the cost benefits are not always passed on to the consumer. Additionally the consumer may not benefit from an improved quality of customer service. Outsourcing decreases prices in another way – the competitive marketplace in which service provider companies operate gets squeezed.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-204"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Low wage countries may need to enhance their infrastructure to attract outsourcing contracts.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"The passage says outsourcing profits encourage underdeveloped countries to invest in the “educational infrastructure and skills training that are required to support such business”.","passage":"Outsourcing – purchasing services from an external supplier rather than performing the work internally – is a popular but politically sensitive means of cutting costs. There has been an increasing use of third parties for HR functions, such as managing payroll and other employee data, and for traditional Finance functions, such as invoice services. The manufacture of goods has even become part of this trend, though the design function is typically kept in-house. Third party call centre operatives can offer customer service expertise that may be more expensive to provide in-house. “Offshoring”, when functions are moved abroad, often to India or China, where the average wage is considerably lower, raises job protection issues. The potential profits from outsourcing operations encourage underdeveloped countries to invest in the necessary educational infrastructure and skills training that are required to support such business. Still, higher corporate profits may be seen to be at the expense of low-wage economies, and the cost benefits are not always passed on to the consumer. Additionally the consumer may not benefit from an improved quality of customer service. Outsourcing decreases prices in another way – the competitive marketplace in which service provider companies operate gets squeezed.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-205"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"Outsourcing providers compete aggressively for client contracts.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":0,"exp":"The passage describes “the competitive marketplace in which service provider companies operate” being “squeezed”, which drives prices down. This is aggressive competition between providers.","passage":"Outsourcing – purchasing services from an external supplier rather than performing the work internally – is a popular but politically sensitive means of cutting costs. There has been an increasing use of third parties for HR functions, such as managing payroll and other employee data, and for traditional Finance functions, such as invoice services. The manufacture of goods has even become part of this trend, though the design function is typically kept in-house. Third party call centre operatives can offer customer service expertise that may be more expensive to provide in-house. “Offshoring”, when functions are moved abroad, often to India or China, where the average wage is considerably lower, raises job protection issues. The potential profits from outsourcing operations encourage underdeveloped countries to invest in the necessary educational infrastructure and skills training that are required to support such business. Still, higher corporate profits may be seen to be at the expense of low-wage economies, and the cost benefits are not always passed on to the consumer. Additionally the consumer may not benefit from an improved quality of customer service. Outsourcing decreases prices in another way – the competitive marketplace in which service provider companies operate gets squeezed.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-206"},{"cat":"Verbal Reasoning","q":"The outsourcing trend has led to a reduction in the cost of consumer goods.","opts":["True","False","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"The passage says cost benefits “are not always passed on to the consumer” and that outsourcing lowers the prices charged by service providers. It never says whether consumer goods have become cheaper.","passage":"Outsourcing – purchasing services from an external supplier rather than performing the work internally – is a popular but politically sensitive means of cutting costs. There has been an increasing use of third parties for HR functions, such as managing payroll and other employee data, and for traditional Finance functions, such as invoice services. The manufacture of goods has even become part of this trend, though the design function is typically kept in-house. Third party call centre operatives can offer customer service expertise that may be more expensive to provide in-house. “Offshoring”, when functions are moved abroad, often to India or China, where the average wage is considerably lower, raises job protection issues. The potential profits from outsourcing operations encourage underdeveloped countries to invest in the necessary educational infrastructure and skills training that are required to support such business. Still, higher corporate profits may be seen to be at the expense of low-wage economies, and the cost benefits are not always passed on to the consumer. Additionally the consumer may not benefit from an improved quality of customer service. Outsourcing decreases prices in another way – the competitive marketplace in which service provider companies operate gets squeezed.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-207"},{"cat":"Verbal Ability","q":"Her “reproaches cut him to the quick”. This means that he","opts":["Was made to eat quickly","Was wounded","Was deeply hurt","Began to speak angrily","Was tortured"],"ans":2,"exp":"To “cut someone to the quick” is to hurt their feelings deeply. (“The quick” is the sensitive flesh under the nails.)","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently. The source prints “approaches”; “reproaches” is the intended word.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-208"},{"cat":"Verbal Ability","q":"Nonso’s father gave him “a free hand” to run the family business. This means that his father","opts":["Assisted him freely","Allowed him to make his own decisions","Made him sole owner of the business","Allowed him freedom of movement in the premises","Made him leave the business"],"ans":1,"exp":"To give someone a free hand is to let them act as they think best, without interference.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-209"},{"cat":"Verbal Ability","q":"The lady swallowed the whole story “hook, line and sinker”. This means that the lady","opts":["Totally delivered the story","Totally believed the story","Had her doubts about the story","Thoroughly enjoyed the story","Totally disbelieved the story"],"ans":1,"exp":"To swallow something “hook, line and sinker” is to believe it completely, like a fish swallowing the whole fishing tackle.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-210"},{"cat":"Verbal Ability","q":"The government has approved a new salary structure as “an incentive” for workers.","opts":["A reward","An encouragement","A package","An advance","A promise"],"ans":1,"exp":"An incentive is something that motivates or encourages people to act — an encouragement. A reward comes after the effort; an incentive comes before it.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently. Choose the option closest in meaning to the expression in quotation marks.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-211"},{"cat":"Verbal Ability","q":"She was “reprimanded” by her boss for negligence.","opts":["Sacked","Rebuked","Punished","Surcharged","Charged"],"ans":1,"exp":"To reprimand is to rebuke someone formally — a scolding, not a dismissal or penalty.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently. Choose the option closest in meaning to the expression in quotation marks.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-212"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Which month has the lowest asset turnover value? (Asset Turnover = Total Sales ÷ Fixed Assets)","opts":["January","February","March","April","May"],"ans":3,"exp":"Jan 136,000 ÷ 38,000 = 3.58; Feb 135,000 ÷ 34,000 = 3.97; Mar 136,500 ÷ 32,000 = 4.27; Apr 156,000 ÷ 45,000 = 3.47; May 145,000 ÷ 40,000 = 3.63. April is lowest.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf5_financials.png","imgLabel":"Monthly financials, January–May (£)","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-213"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Compared to May’s figures, Total Sales for June show an increase of 8% and Operating Expenses show a decrease of 7%. What is June’s Income?","opts":["£105,450","£95,450","£85,450","£75,450","Can’t tell from the data"],"ans":0,"exp":"June sales = 145,000 × 1.08 = 156,600. June expenses = 55,000 × 0.93 = 51,150. Income = 156,600 − 51,150 = £105,450.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf5_financials.png","imgLabel":"Monthly financials, January–May (£)","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-214"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Which month has the highest Working Capital to Total Assets ratio? (Ratio = (Current Assets − Liabilities) ÷ Total Assets)","opts":["January","February","March","April","May"],"ans":2,"exp":"Jan 32,100 ÷ 140,500 = 0.228; Feb 27,400 ÷ 132,500 = 0.207; Mar 32,000 ÷ 133,000 = 0.241; Apr 33,000 ÷ 151,000 = 0.219; May 32,500 ÷ 142,500 = 0.228. March is highest.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf5_financials.png","imgLabel":"Monthly financials, January–May (£)","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-215"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If the average value of Total Assets between the months of April to June is £150,000, what is the value of Total Assets in June?","opts":["£154,500","£155,000","£155,500","£156,000","£156,500"],"ans":4,"exp":"April–June total = 3 × 150,000 = 450,000. June = 450,000 − 151,000 − 142,500 = £156,500.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf5_financials.png","imgLabel":"Monthly financials, January–May (£)","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-216"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If the average monthly sales for the first five months of the year was the same for the months of June to December, what was the total annual sales?","opts":["£1,500,400","£1,600,400","£1,700,400","£1,800,400","£1,900,400"],"ans":2,"exp":"Jan–May sales = 136,000 + 135,000 + 136,500 + 156,000 + 145,000 = 708,500, so the monthly average is 141,700. Annual sales = 12 × 141,700 = £1,700,400.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf5_financials.png","imgLabel":"Monthly financials, January–May (£)","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-217"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"For which laptop, or laptops, is the difference between the manufacturing cost and the design cost less than 20% of the manufacturing cost?","opts":["Brete","Stunn and Adelphi","Adelphi","Stunn","None of these"],"ans":4,"exp":"Difference vs 20% of manufacturing cost: Adelphi 105 vs 33; Adele 50 vs 28; Faze 60 vs 24; Stunn 30 vs 29; Brete 65 vs 39. Every difference is larger than 20%. Stunn is the closest but still misses by £1.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf5_laptops.png","imgLabel":"Laptop costs and prices (£)","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-218"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Put the laptop models in order of increasing mark-up (Mark-up = UK Price − Costs).","opts":["Adele, Adelphi, Stunn, Faze, Brete","Adele, Stunn, Brete, Adelphi, Faze","Adele, Stunn, Adelphi, Faze, Brete","Stunn, Adele, Adelphi, Brete, Faze","Adele, Stunn, Adelphi, Brete, Faze"],"ans":2,"exp":"Mark-ups: Adele 350 − 230 = 120; Stunn 420 − 260 = 160; Adelphi 400 − 225 = 175; Faze 380 − 180 = 200; Brete 650 − 325 = 325.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf5_laptops.png","imgLabel":"Laptop costs and prices (£)","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-219"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If the same number of each model was sold last month and total sales were £220,000, how many of each model were sold?","opts":["200","2,510","100","2,150","Cannot say"],"ans":2,"exp":"One of each model at UK prices = 400 + 350 + 380 + 420 + 650 = £2,200. 220,000 ÷ 2,200 = 100 of each. (At sale prices the set costs £1,257.83, which does not divide 220,000 evenly, so normal prices are intended.)","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf5_laptops.png","imgLabel":"Laptop costs and prices (£)","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-220"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Which of the following would generate the highest total amount at the sale prices shown?","opts":["75 Adele laptops on sale","150 Adele laptops at a further 60% reduction to the sale price","50 Faze and 50 Stunn laptops on sale","45 Brete laptops on sale","90 Stunn laptops on sale"],"ans":0,"exp":"Sale prices: Adele 3/4 × 350 = 262.50; Faze 2/5 × 380 = 152; Stunn 1/2 × 420 = 210; Brete 2/3 × 650 = 433.33. A: 75 × 262.50 = £19,687.50. B: 150 × 105 = £15,750. C: 7,600 + 10,500 = £18,100. D: 45 × 433.33 = £19,500. E: 90 × 210 = £18,900.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf5_laptops.png","imgLabel":"Laptop costs and prices (£)","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-221"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If the sale price for a Faze laptop is $182.40 in the United States and €255.36 in France, what is the sale price ratio for UK : US : France? (Use exchange rates of €1.20 to the £ and $1.50 to the £.)","opts":["152:121:212","7:6:9","5:4:7","4:5:7","152:122:213"],"ans":2,"exp":"UK sale price = 2/5 × 380 = £152. US = 182.40 ÷ 1.5 = £121.60. France = 255.36 ÷ 1.2 = £212.80. 152 : 121.6 : 212.8 — divide by 30.4 to get 5 : 4 : 7.","img":"assets/img/workforce/wf5_laptops.png","imgLabel":"Laptop costs and prices (£)","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-222"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"Judy is now twice as old as Adam, but 6 years ago she was 5 times as old as he was. How old is Judy now?","opts":["10","16","20","24","32"],"ans":1,"exp":"Let Adam = A, so Judy = 2A. 2A − 6 = 5(A − 6), so 3A = 24 and A = 8. Judy = 16.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-223"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"If 25 students took an exam and 4 of them failed, what percent of them passed?","opts":["84","88","90","100","102"],"ans":0,"exp":"21 passed. 21 ÷ 25 = 84%.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-224"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"The price of a can of soup was increased by 20%. How many cans can be purchased for the amount of money that used to buy 300 cans?","opts":["200","240","250","300","350"],"ans":2,"exp":"Each can now costs 1.2 times as much: 300 ÷ 1.2 = 250 cans.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-225"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"A hospital has enough pills on hand to treat 10 patients for 14 days. How long will the pills last if there are 35 patients?","opts":["4 days","5 days","6 days","7 days","8 days"],"ans":0,"exp":"Supply = 10 × 14 = 140 patient-days. 140 ÷ 35 = 4 days.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-226"},{"cat":"Numerical Reasoning","q":"John can read 72 pages per hour. At this rate, how many pages can he read in 72 minutes?","opts":["68.4","86.4","88.4","89.2","92.4"],"ans":1,"exp":"72 minutes = 1.2 hours. 72 × 1.2 = 86.4 pages.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-227"},{"cat":"General Knowledge","q":"A banking institution that avoids earning interest from its customers is the","opts":["Islamic Bank","Arabic Bank","Central Bank","Regional Bank","Microfinance Bank"],"ans":0,"exp":"Islamic (non-interest) banks follow Sharia, which forbids interest (riba). They earn returns through profit-sharing, leasing and trade arrangements instead.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-228"},{"cat":"General Knowledge","q":"A bank that provides liquidity to the banking system and acts as the lender of last resort in the event of a crisis is the","opts":["Central Bank","National Bank","World Bank","Commercial Bank","Continental Bank"],"ans":0,"exp":"Acting as lender of last resort to other banks is a core function of a central bank, e.g. the CBN in Nigeria.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently. The printed options did not include the correct answer; “SenateBank” has been replaced with “Central Bank”.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-229"},{"cat":"General Knowledge","q":"After the first special examination of banks in 2009, five banks were found to be in a grave condition. How much did the CBN inject to improve their liquidity?","opts":["₦620 billion","₦500 billion","₦420 billion","₦300 billion","₦220 billion"],"ans":2,"exp":"In August 2009 the CBN removed the management of five banks (Afribank, Intercontinental, Oceanic, Union and Finbank) and injected ₦420 billion into them. A further ₦200 billion went to three more banks in October, bringing the total to ₦620 billion.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently. ₦620 billion is the combined total for all eight banks; the question asks about the first five.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-230"},{"cat":"General Knowledge","q":"Dr J. O. Sanusi was Governor of the CBN during which period?","opts":["1992–1997","1997–2002","1999–2004","2000–2005","2009–2013"],"ans":2,"exp":"Joseph Oladele Sanusi was CBN Governor from 1999 to 2004. He was succeeded by Chukwuma Soludo. (Not to be confused with Sanusi Lamido Sanusi, 2009–2014.)","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-231"},{"cat":"General Knowledge","q":"Tom was ineligible to vote at the general elections because he was under the age of eighteen. This makes him a","opts":["Criminal","Offender of the law","Minor","Major","Convict"],"ans":2,"exp":"A minor is a person below the legal age of majority (18 in Nigeria).","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-232"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Families with 2–3 children are more likely to own a cat than are members of any other demographic group. The popular belief that single, old ladies are more likely to be feeding cats than a family must therefore be false.\n\nThere is a problem in the argument because…","opts":["The author does not supply any information about demographic groups other than families with 2–3 children","The author depends on a popular belief rather than documented research","The author does not specify the precise types and ages of cats involved","The author fails to point out the difference between owning cats and feeding possibly stray cats","The author might be working for a cat-food factory"],"ans":3,"exp":"The evidence is about owning cats but the belief being rejected is about feeding cats. Old ladies could feed strays without owning them, so the evidence does not disprove the belief.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-233"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Last year, inflation in Canada stood at 2%, but this year inflation is just 0.7%. However, this downward trend might stop, and inflation might go back up next year.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, most strengthens the conclusion?","opts":["Inflation figures are calculated using a sample of economic data, and not all the available data","Low inflation figures generally last for periods of at least 3 years","Government intervention cannot affect the rate of inflation to any significant degree","The 0.7% figure represents a 15-year low in Canada","The economic crisis, which lowered inflation figures this year, is expected to be over before next year starts"],"ans":4,"exp":"If the cause of this year’s low inflation is about to disappear, inflation is likely to rise again. B weakens the conclusion, and the others do not bear on next year’s direction.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-234"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"In the 1940s, the introduction of the 33 rpm long-playing vinyl record completely changed the way we listen to music. The breakable, three-minute 78 rpm record soon disappeared from the marketplace. In our day, the compact disc, superior in quality and convenience, has replaced the vinyl long-playing record and will . . .\n\nWhich of the following is the most logical completion of the passage?","opts":["increase the size of the record-buying public","increase the profits of the record industry","drive the 78 rpm record from the second-hand market","make the manufacture of phonographs that play 33 rpm records unnecessary","encourage the growth of computer-generated music"],"ans":3,"exp":"The passage is about one format making the previous one obsolete. If CDs replace 33 rpm records, the players built for those records will no longer be needed (D). The other options bring in ideas the passage never discusses.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-235"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Most people who betray their country through some form of espionage are driven by irrational desires to feel powerful or important, to seek revenge, or even to take risks. If these people were thinking rationally, they would not be involved in such self-destructive behaviour.\n\nThe argument above rests on which of the following assumptions?","opts":["Self-destruction is the most frequent result of espionage","The desire to feel powerful is always irrational","Those involved in espionage do not have rational reasons for their behaviour","Espionage is rarely a successful way to gain revenge","Risk-taking is a less frequent motivation for espionage than the desire to feel important"],"ans":2,"exp":"Saying that rational thinkers would not spy assumes there are no rational reasons for spying (C). B is too absolute (“always”) and is not needed, because the argument already calls these particular desires irrational.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-236"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Fran: I want to stay out of Professor Caldwell’s classes if I can. I’ve heard she’s very strict when it comes to giving out grades.\nSid: That’s not true. My friend Phil took her class last year, and she gave him an A.\n\nIt can be inferred that Sid interpreted Fran’s statement to mean that Professor Caldwell…","opts":["makes unfair demands on her students","only gives good grades to a few favoured students","has become increasingly strict in her grading over the past year","gives out fewer good grades than most teachers in the department","never gives out grades of A"],"ans":4,"exp":"Sid thinks one A is enough to refute Fran. A single counter-example only disproves an absolute claim, so he took her to mean the professor never gives an A.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-237"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"In a laboratory study, 160 rabbits in an experimental group were injected with serum D, while 160 rabbits in a control group were injected with a harmless sugar solution. Within two weeks, 39% of the experimental group rabbits had contracted jungle fever, a highly contagious and usually fatal disease. Therefore, jungle fever must be caused by some substance similar to the substance found in serum D.\n\nThe argument above would be most greatly strengthened if it were shown that","opts":["The normal rate of jungle fever among rabbits is less than 0.01%","40% of the rabbits in the control group had also contracted jungle fever within two weeks","Serum D contains substances extracted from the root of a certain poisonous jungle wildflower","The blood of jungle fever victims invariably contains a high level of a certain toxic substance also found in serum D","Nearly all the rabbits who contracted jungle fever died within two days of the appearance of the first symptoms"],"ans":3,"exp":"The conclusion is about a specific substance in serum D. Finding that substance in every jungle fever victim (D) links it directly to the disease. A only suggests serum D somehow raised the rate. B would weaken the argument.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-238"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"The high level of violence in children’s television programming today has often been cited as an explanation for the increased violence in our society as a whole. And in fact, some recent studies show that the level of TV violence has increased considerably over the past twenty years. However, other studies indicate that the level, while high, is only slightly greater than it was twenty years ago.\n\nAll of the following, if true, would be useful in explaining the above EXCEPT","opts":["Numerous studies of TV violence have been conducted in the past twenty years, and their results were not always in agreement","All those involved in conducting the studies cited had the same perception of what constitutes “violence” in TV programming","Despite their best efforts at impartiality, those who conducted studies of TV violence sometimes allowed their preconceived ideas to affect their findings","Many factors other than TV violence have a significant effect on the level of violence in society","The methodology generally used in studies of TV violence has changed considerably over the past twenty years"],"ans":1,"exp":"A, C and E give reasons why studies could disagree, and D explains why societal violence could rise even if TV violence barely changed. B removes a possible source of disagreement (different definitions of violence), so it makes the conflicting results harder to explain.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-239"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Prior to the development of the “horseless carriage” around the start of the twentieth century, horses in our cities left tons of unsightly, messy and malodorous manure on our streets. Based on this fact alone there is no question that by any measure, the automobile has been a boon to humankind.\n\nWhich of the following, if true, most seriously weakens the argument above?","opts":["Air pollution caused by automobile exhaust is less deleterious to health than that caused by horse manure","In the nineteenth century, almost as many people were killed each year by horse-drawn conveyances as are killed today by automobiles","In many cities, automobile traffic has been banned from large downtown areas to provide room for pedestrian malls","Compared to horse-drawn carriages, automobiles are less efficient in terms of the energy required to operate them","Automobiles enable people to travel greater distances in less time than did the horse-drawn conveyances"],"ans":3,"exp":"The argument claims the car is better “by any measure”. D gives a measure on which cars are worse — energy efficiency — which directly breaks that claim. A and E support the argument. B is inconclusive, because today’s population and travel are far larger.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-240"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"At the end of the nineteenth century, most Americans lived in houses in rural areas and dogs were by far the most popular type of pet. Today, however, the majority of Americans live in urban apartments and more people keep cats than any other animal.\n\nWhich of the following conclusions is most strongly supported by the statements above?","opts":["City life is more congenial than country life for people and their pets","Cats are more appropriate as pets for apartment dwellers than are dogs","Today, as compared to the past, more people prefer having cats as pets than dogs","Dogs who live in city apartments are often unhealthy and unhappy","Dogs have decreased in popularity due to city ordinances restricting the keeping of pets in apartments"],"ans":2,"exp":"The statements only report a shift in which pet is most often kept, from dogs to cats. C restates that shift. A, B, D and E each add a cause or a value judgement that the passage does not support.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-241"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Mary: All of the graduates from Midland High School go to State College.\nAnn: I don’t know. Some of the students at State College come from North Hills High School.\n\nAnn’s response shows that she has interpreted Mary’s remark to mean that","opts":["Most of the students from North Hills High School attend State College","None of the students at State College are from Midland High School","Only students from Midland High School attend State College","Midland High School is a better school than North Hills High School","Some Midland High School graduates do not attend college"],"ans":2,"exp":"Ann’s point (students also come from North Hills) only contradicts Mary if Mary meant that State College admits only Midland graduates. Ann has reversed “all Midland graduates go to State” into “only Midland graduates go to State”.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-242"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"All students have submitted applications for admission. Some of the applications for admission have not been acted upon; therefore some more students will be accepted.\n\nThe logic of which of the following is most similar to that of the argument above?","opts":["Some of the barrels have not yet been loaded on the truck, but all of the apples have been put into barrels. So, some more apples will be loaded onto the truck.","All students who received passing marks were juniors. X received a passing mark. Therefore, X is a junior.","Some chemicals will react with glass bottles, but not with plastic bottles. Therefore, those chemicals should be kept in plastic bottles and not glass ones.","All advertising must be approved by the Council before it is aired. This television spot for a new cola has not yet been approved by the Council. Therefore, it is not to be aired until the Council makes its decision.","There are six blue marbles and three red marbles in this jar. Therefore, if I blindly pick out seven marbles, there should be two marbles left to pick."],"ans":0,"exp":"The pattern is: all X are in containers Y; some Y have not yet been processed; so more X will be processed. A matches exactly (apples → barrels → loaded).","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-243"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Some Alphas are not Gammas. All Betas are Gammas.\n\nWhich of the following conclusions can be deduced from the two statements above?","opts":["Some Alphas are not Betas","No Gammas are Alphas","All Gammas are Betas","All Alphas are Gammas","Some Alphas are Gammas"],"ans":0,"exp":"Every Beta is a Gamma, so anything that is not a Gamma cannot be a Beta. The Alphas that are not Gammas are therefore not Betas.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-244"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"Ali: If an alien species ever visited Earth, it would surely be because they were looking for other intelligent species with whom they could communicate. Since we have not been contacted by aliens, we may conclude that none have ever visited this planet.\nAmy: Or, perhaps, they did not think human beings intelligent.\n\nHow is Amy’s response related to Ali’s argument?","opts":["She misses Ali’s point entirely","She attacks Ali personally rather than his reasoning","She points out that Ali made an unwarranted assumption","She ignores the detailed internal development of Ali’s logic","She introduces a false analogy"],"ans":2,"exp":"Ali assumes that visiting aliens would have found us intelligent and contacted us. Amy points out another explanation for the lack of contact, which exposes that assumption.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-245"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"I maintain that the best way to solve our company’s present financial crisis is to bring out a new line of goods. I challenge anyone who disagrees with this proposed course of action to show that it will not work.\n\nA flaw in the preceding argument is that it","opts":["employs group classifications without regard to individuals","introduces an analogy which is weak","attempts to shift the burden of proof to those who would object to the plan","fails to provide statistical evidence to show that the plan will actually succeed","relies upon a discredited economic theory"],"ans":2,"exp":"The speaker gives no support for the plan and instead demands that critics prove it will fail. This shifts the burden of proof.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-246"},{"cat":"Critical Reasoning","q":"I. Whenever some of the runners are leading off and all of the infielders are playing in, all of the batters attempt to bunt.\nII. Some of the runners are leading off but some of the batters are not attempting to bunt.\n\nWhich of the following conclusions can be deduced from the two statements above?","opts":["Some of the runners are not leading off","Some of the batters are attempting to bunt","None of the infielders is playing in","All of the infielders are playing in","Some of the infielders are not playing in"],"ans":4,"exp":"Statement I: (some runners lead off AND all infielders in) → all batters bunt. Not all batters bunt, so the condition fails. Runners are leading off, so the part that fails must be “all infielders in”. Therefore some infielders are not playing in.","note":"This paper has no published answer key; the answer shown was worked out independently.","src":"Workforce Test 5.0","id":"workforce-247"}];
